{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat docs/decisions/0004-tooltip-and-focused-text.md && ls docs/decisions && rg -n '\"'^#|'\"\\\\| (I[1-6]|A[0-9]+|L[2678]|C1) ' docs/02-product-spec.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Decision 0004: where the tooltip goes, and when the focused point's text changes\n\nStatus: decided by the coordinator on 2026-11-18 after the second review of the interaction layer (`reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core3.interaction.review.md`, new findings 3 and 4). Not rehearsed with a screen reader or a screen magnifier; both parts need that before they are called verified.\n\n## 1. The tooltip is anchored to the datum and never takes the pointer (revision 2)\n\nRevision 1 (placed across the reading axis, offset from the pointer, interactive while hovered) was implemented three times and still failed the acceptance tests: because the tooltip's position followed the pointer it moved away when approached, because it took pointer events it stalled the readout or swallowed the click whenever a move landed on it, and it could cover controls and the focus ring. Revision 2 removes the causes instead of tuning distances.\n\n**Problem.** WCAG 1.4.13 requires content shown on hover to stay when the pointer moves onto it. Spec I1 requires the readout to name the datum at the pointer on every move, the tooltip not to cover the active mark, to stay inside the viewport, and to fall back to a fixed readout when it cannot fit. On a line chart the whole plot picks, so anything that takes pointer events inside the plot blocks picking.\n\n**Rules.**\n\n1. **Position depends on the datum only.** For the datum on show, the tooltip is placed against the bounding box of that datum's active marks (all series at that x for line, area and bar; the point for scatter; the slice for donut), enlarged by the focus ring's width. The pointer's own position never enters the computation, so the tooltip does not move while the datum is unchanged, and it cannot run away from an approaching pointer.\n2. **Candidates, in order:** above the box, below it, then to the right and to the left of it (for horizontal bars: right, left, above, below). Along the other axis it is centred on the box and shifted only as far as needed. A candidate is valid when the tooltip lies wholly inside the plot rectangle, inside the viewport and inside every clipping ancestor, and overlaps neither the box nor any control. The first valid candidate is used. For a donut the first candidate is the hole, when the tooltip fits inside it.\n3. **A hovered tooltip never takes pointer events.** It is `pointer-events: none`. The plot keeps receiving every move, so picking never stalls at any speed, and a click goes to the plot and selects the datum under the pointer. Moving the pointer onto the tooltip therefore cannot hide it: on line, area and bar the tooltip stays for as long as the pointer's position along the reading axis still picks the same datum, which a straight approach across the reading axis does; on scatter and donut, while the pointer is inside the tooltip's rectangle the datum is held (computed from coordinates in the plot's own move handler, not by letting the tooltip take the event).\n3b. **A rest holds the datum along the way to the tooltip.** Rule 3 is enough when the tooltip sits across the reading axis (above or below), because a straight approach does not change what is picked. It is not enough when the straight path from the datum to its tooltip crosses positions that pick something else: a tooltip beside a tall bar or stack, a donut's tooltip in the hole or outside the ring, a scatter point's tooltip. For those: once the pointer has rested on a datum for 250 ms (no move larger than 2 CSS px), that datum is held while the pointer stays inside the corridor between its resting position and the tooltip's rectangle (the convex hull of the point and the rectangle) and inside the rectangle itself. Leaving the corridor, or leaving the rectangle on any side but back into the corridor, resumes picking at once. A pointer that has not rested is never held, so a sweep across the plot never stalls, whatever its speed. Placements across the reading axis never use the corridor.\n4. **Rows are not controls while hovered.** A hovered tooltip is text. Clicking or tapping the plot pins the tooltip for that datum; a pinned tooltip takes pointer events, stays until Escape, a click elsewhere, or its datum leaves the displayed frame, and its rows are buttons of at least 24 × 24 CSS px (WCAG 2.5.8) that select a series. Keyboard focus shows the same tooltip by the same placement; it is never over the focus ring because the box includes the ring.\n5. **Readout mode is decided by layout, not by hover.** When the plot is narrower than 240 CSS px or shorter than 140 CSS px, the figure is in readout mode: a strip of two text lines is reserved under the plot in the layout, and the content that would be in the tooltip is written there as running text (\"x: series value; series value; +N more\"), wrapping to the two lines and ending in \"+N more\" when it does not fit. The strip is part of the figure's flow: it covers nothing and nothing covers it. In readout mode there is no floating tooltip. A pinned datum makes the strip's entries buttons of 24 px height.\n6. **When no candidate is valid outside readout mode** (a tooltip taller than the room around a tall stack, for example), rows are dropped from the end and counted in \"+N more\" until a candidate is valid; if none is with one row, the tooltip is placed inside the plot at the candidate that overlaps the box least. This is the only case in which the active mark may be partly covered, and the table still has every value.\n7. Escape dismisses a hovered or pinned tooltip without moving the pointer, until the pointer reaches a different datum.\n\nRejected: revision 1; an interactive hovered tooltip with a \"safe triangle\" (still ambiguous when the reading axis and the approach coincide); always using the readout (loses the association between the values and the mark on plots that have room).\n\nWhat \"can itself be hovered\" (spec I1) means under this decision, for the tests: the pointer can be moved straight from the datum onto the tooltip's rectangle, and the tooltip is still shown, for the same datum, when the pointer is inside it. It does not mean the tooltip is the hit-test target.\n\n## 2. The focused point's text changes only for a reason the reader would name\n\n**Problem.** The plot's keyboard proxy is a native range whose value text describes the focused point, ending with its position (\"Point 4 of 120\"). While data streams in, the count changes on every append, so the text of a focused control changes without the reader acting. Spec A7 forbids unrequested announcements; whether a given screen reader speaks such a change cannot be tested here.\n\n**Rule.** The range's bounds and numeric value are always kept true to the displayed frame, silently. Its value text is rewritten only when:\n\n1. the reader moves (a key, an assistive adjustment, a pointer or touch selection that takes focus),\n2. the focused datum's own values change (the same x and series now read differently),\n3. the focused datum leaves the frame (the text must then say where focus went), or\n4. the plot regains focus.\n\nAn append that only changes the count or the ordinal of an unchanged datum does not rewrite the text. The position in the text can therefore be out of date until the next of the four events; it is recomputed then.\n\nTo check with a screen reader when one is available (added to decision 0001's list): idle appends with the plot focused are silent; an eviction of the focused point is spoken once; an increment immediately after an append lands on the expected neighbour and reads the current count.\n0001-plot-interaction-pattern.md\n0002-mark-renderer.md\n0003-default-palette.md\n0004-tooltip-and-focused-text.md\n1:# Phase 1b: Axylo product specification\n7:## 1. What Axylo is\n15:### 1.1 Why someone would choose it\n29:### 1.2 Who it is for\n35:### 1.3 Principles\n46:## 2. Scope of the first release\n48:### 2.1 Chart types\n65:### 2.2 Workflows\n76:### 2.3 Supported environments\n86:## 3. Acceptance criteria: the reader's experience\n90:### 3.1 Faithful representation\n107:### 3.2 Legibility\n112:| L2 | Text is at least 12 CSS px and at least 4.5:1 against what is behind it, including text drawn inside a mark and legend entries for hidden series. Marks, axis lines and focus indicators are at least 3:1. Gridlines are exempt: they are not needed to read a value while the tooltip and table exist. Holds in light and dark. | Computed-style and rendered-pixel checks in both schemes | R7, R10 |\n116:| L6 | Title, subtitle and axis titles with units are part of the chart. A value axis is titled with its label (the field name when none is given) and unit. An x axis that is a measure (number) is titled the same way; a time, date or category x axis is titled only when the author gives a `label`, since its ticks already say what it is. A reference line or band shows its label next to it. Axis titles never overlap tick labels. | Layout tests; `trend` fixture | R9 |\n117:| L7 | Empty data shows a message inside the plot area; so do loading, error and Axylo's own internal failures. No invented axis ranges, no blank box. A change of state is announced once, politely. | `empty` fixture: message visible and in the accessibility tree, no tick labels; forced internal error shows the error state | R6 |\n118:| L8 | When the figure cannot hold a readable plot (less than 120×80 CSS px for the plot), it shows the title, a short summary (the first sentence of the generated summary, on at most two lines, cut with an ellipsis beyond that; the full summary remains the figure's description) and the table control instead, and raises AX142. | Narrow and short container tests | R2 |\n120:### 3.3 Interaction\n124:| I1 | Pointing anywhere in the plot shows what is there, by the picking rule for the chart type (section 3.6), within one frame at the 95th percentile. On line, area and bar the tooltip lists every visible series at the nearest x; a series with no row at that x reads \"No observation\", distinct from \"No data\". The tooltip stays inside the viewport, does not cover the active mark or any control, can itself be hovered, and lists at most eight rows plus \"+N more\". In a plot too small for it (decision 0004: narrower than 240 px or shorter than 140 px) the same content appears as text in a strip reserved under the plot. Escape closes it until the pointer reaches a different datum. | Hover at 20 positions including all four edges; sparse-series fixture; narrow tile with eight series; Escape test | R15 |\n125:| I2 | Legend entries are toggle buttons with a pressed state, operable by click, Enter and Space. | Keyboard and accessibility-tree tests | R12 |\n126:| I3 | Zoom on time, date and numeric x axes is reachable four ways: visible Zoom in, Zoom out and Reset buttons that work with single clicks; drag to select a range; `+`, `-`, `0` and Shift+arrows; and pinch when `zoom: 'touch'` is set. The wheel zooms only while the plot has focus, and never when Ctrl or Meta is held, so browser zoom and page scrolling are not intercepted. | Reach a given range by clicks only; by keyboard only; by drag; page scrolls normally with the pointer over an unfocused chart | D7, R12, WCAG 2.5.1, 2.5.7, 1.4.4 |\n127:| I4 | Every interaction works with a single pointer without dragging, and by touch. Every control is at least 24×24 CSS px. A one-finger drag on a chart scrolls the page. | Touch emulation; size checks | WCAG 2.5.7, 2.5.8 |\n128:| I5 | First render is not animated. An update may transition for at most 200 ms. Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` nothing transitions, on first render or on update. | After `document.fonts.ready`: first frame equals the frame at 2.5 s within a 0.1% pixel tolerance, in both settings; an update under reduced motion reaches its final frame in one step; a 24-chart filter change settles within 200 ms | R14 |\n129:| I6 | A chart fed by `append` shows a Pause/Resume control. While paused, new rows are buffered and the view does not move. | Stream test | WCAG 2.2.2 |\n131:### 3.4 Accessibility\n137:| A1 | Every chart has an accessible name: its `title`, or the heading named by `labelledBy`, or else a generated name (\"Line chart of Sessions by Day\") with AX140 raised in every build. The title's heading level is the author's (`level`), and it can be hidden visually. Every chart has a generated summary: type, series, x range with time zone, y range, each series' minimum, maximum and last value, and any omissions. `description` adds to the summary and never removes the zone or omission statements. Element ids are unique per chart. | Accessibility-tree snapshot for every fixture; a 24-chart page has no duplicate ids; axe `heading-order` | R11, R13 |\n138:| A2 | Keyboard: at most two tab stops while the table is closed, the plot and one toolbar with roving focus that holds legend toggles, zoom, pause and the table control. Every point is reachable in order; the series can be switched; the ends can be reached directly; focus can leave the chart. The focus indicator is at least 2 px and 3:1 and is not hidden by the tooltip. The exact keys and roles are fixed after the pattern spike in M1 (section 8). | Forward and reverse traversal in collapsed, zoomed, streaming and table-open states; focus after hiding the focused series; indicator contrast | R12 |\n139:| A3 | Moving focus to a point conveys its text (series, x, value with unit, position) through the focused element's accessible name or a polite live region. On touch screen readers, every point is reachable by swiping, or the table is reachable within two gestures. | Accessibility-tree snapshot after each key; a recorded rehearsal on NVDA with Chrome, NVDA with Firefox, VoiceOver with Safari on macOS, VoiceOver on iOS, and TalkBack with Chrome | R12, R13 |\n140:| A4 | \"View as table\" shows the data as an HTML table built from the normalized data when opened: the title as caption, field labels with units as column headers, x values as row headers, values formatted as in F6. It covers the current x range, 100 rows per page, with every row reachable by paging or by jumping to an x value. It sits in a focusable scroll region no taller than the plot, and opening it does not move other charts. | Table content compared with fixture data, including a value near row 900,000 of 1,000,000; a 24-chart page layout test | R13 |\n141:| A5 | Series are never distinguished by color alone, in any mode. Each line and area carries a shape marker at its last point and at sparse intervals, repeated in the legend and tooltip; under `prefers-contrast: more` or forced colors, lines also differ by dash. Adjacent filled marks (slices, stacked segments) are separated by at least 1 px of background. The default palette has eight colors, each at least 3:1 on the default light and dark backgrounds, with a minimum pairwise OKLab difference of 0.10 (provisional) under normal vision and simulated protanopia, deuteranopia and tritanopia. On any other background, Axylo measures the palette against the resolved background and raises AX141 below 3:1. | Palette script scoring the rendered marks of an eight-series chart in both schemes, repeats included; rendered-pixel checks of separators; a grayscale screenshot in which three crossing lines can be told apart by marker | R7, WCAG 1.4.1, 1.4.11 |\n142:| A6 | Content reflows at 320 CSS px and 400% zoom without two-dimensional scrolling. User text-spacing overrides do not clip text. | Viewport and style-override tests in three engines | WCAG 1.4.10, 1.4.12 |\n143:| A7 | Data arriving does not speak. Announcements happen only for the reader's own navigation, and for a change of state (L7). While data streams, keyboard focus stays on the same datum until it leaves the window. | Stream test counting live-region changes with and without focus; 24 concurrent streams | R12 |\n144:| A8 | axe-core reports no violations on any fixture in any state, including `heading-order` and `scrollable-region-focusable`. | CI | R11 |\n145:| A9 | A conformance table lists every applicable WCAG 2.2 AA success criterion, the test that covers it, and what the author must supply (a meaningful title or `labelledBy`, series names, units, the page's own structure). It also states the limits in section 3.8. | Published with each release; a CI check that every listed test exists | R11 |\n146:| A10 | Every built-in string can be replaced through `messages`. Built-in English text is marked `lang=\"en\"` when the document language is not English. | Test on a `lang=\"de\"` page | WCAG 3.1.2 |\n148:### 3.5 Diagnostic registry\n188:### 3.6 Type matrix\n200:### 3.7 Compact variant\n206:| C1 | A compact chart is a static image with a full text alternative: `role=\"img\"`, an accessible name (as in A1, including AX140 when none is given) and the generated summary as its description (series, x range, minimum, maximum, last value, omissions). It has no tab stop and no pointer-only behavior, so nothing is available to a mouse that is not available to a keyboard. | Accessibility-tree snapshot; a page of 40 has zero added tab stops |\n214:### 3.8 What the evidence will and will not show\n218:## 4. Acceptance criteria: the developer's experience\n235:## 5. Performance budgets\n256:## 6. Decisions and the findings behind them\n281:## 7. Changes this revision rejected\n290:## 8. Decisions still open\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
