{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '/## 2. Findings/,\"'$p'\"' reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core3.interaction.review.md | sed -E 's@\\\\]\\\\(/home/[\"'^)]*'\"\\\\)@]@g'\"","aggregated_output":"## 2. Findings in the rewritten paths\n\n1. **B · An assistive adjustment can hang the page. Ran the steps, reasoned through the loop.** Use scatter rows `A0, B1, A2, B3, A4` in x order. Narrow to B at global ordinal 1, then set the native range to 2 through `input` or `change`. `atOrdinal` repeatedly chooses a direction toward 2 using *series-local* stepping. I bounded the experiment and got `1, 3, 1, 3, 1, 3, 1`. The production loop has no bound. Resolve absolute native values directly in global ordinal order, reconcile narrowed mode afterward, and enforce termination. Platforms that deliver native value changes bypass the key handler. Platforms that synthesize Up/Down instead encounter series switching, so the synthetic DOM-event test cannot settle touch-reader behavior. **A2, A3, decision 0001, WCAG 4.1.2.** [interaction.ts:486] [interaction.ts:530] [focus.ts:331]\n\n2. **B · A right-hand toolbar is covered by its own table. Reasoned.** Set `legend:'right'`, then click View as table. The side toolbar and plot are siblings in `.axylo-body`. The opaque table covers that entire body at `z-index:4`. Only the plot becomes inert. A pointer cannot reach Hide table, while keyboard focus can still land on fully covered controls. Put the table and plot in the same column, keep the toolbar outside the overlay, and move focus into the newly opened region. With the default bottom toolbar, opening by keyboard currently leaves focus on a disclosure *after* the table, so the next Tab leaves the chart. **I4, A2, A4, WCAG 2.4.3 and 2.4.11.** [figure.ts:138] [interaction.ts:1001] [css.ts:236]\n\n3. **M · The clearance rule makes hover accuracy depend on pointer speed. Reasoned.** In a wide plot, hover at `(200,120)` with the mark at that x and room on the right. The tooltip can begin at `x=222` and span `y=120`. Move in one event to `(235,120)`. Hit testing enters the old tooltip, `pointerleave` preserves its target, and the plot receives no move. A click on a row selects the old datum. Conversely, a slow approach can keep moving the tooltip away. The presentation cache also rounds pointer coordinates into 8-pixel buckets, so 22 px is a placement-time clearance, not a continuing guarantee. Use a stable, hoverable area outside the picking surface, with a continuous approach from the plot. A reserved readout can serve that purpose whenever a floating placement would cover selectable plot positions. It can then keep its row actions and Escape behavior. **I1, I4.** [interaction.ts:355] [interaction.ts:732] [tooltip.ts:174] [WCAG 1.4.13](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/#content-on-hover-or-focus)\n\n4. **M · Focused streaming changes the reader’s value without navigation. Ran the text changes. Speech is unverified.** Each distinct-x append in my fixed-focus example changed `aria-valuetext` through its denominator. With bounded `keep`, the ordinal can also change while the same datum survives. Identical strings are guarded, so it would be incorrect to say that every append rewrites the attribute or that every reader announces it. A reader that voices focused value changes on this path would violate A7. Keep native bounds and selection current. Separate changing collection metadata from the focused datum’s unsolicited updates and provide the current position on navigation. Rehearse idle appends, eviction and a user increment immediately after append on all five specified pairs. **A3, A7, decision 0001.** [interaction.ts:410] [focus.ts:113]\n\n5. **M · Data replacement can leave the slider describing a point the plot does not highlight. Reasoned.** Focus the plot with the pointer outside it and call `update({data: …})`. The epoch branch clears `focus`. `writeProxy` computes an initial ref without storing it. `current()` still sees `focus === null`, so the slider describes the new first point while its point ring, tooltip and hover target disappear until another action. Replacement can also suppress an external hover update when the new epoch reuses the old series key and x. Establish one effective focus for the new epoch before writing any presentation, and invalidate hover identity across epochs. **A2, A3, `DatumRef`, plan 1.5.** [interaction.ts:325] [interaction.ts:420] [interaction.ts:1233]\n\n6. **M · Entering the too-small or compact layout loses focus and can leave old decoration. Reasoned.** Focus a point and shrink below the plot threshold. `placeProxy` hides the active input without handing focus to the table disclosure. Switch a hovered or focused chart to `compact:true` and `prepare` nulls `c` before `clear` can call `drawOverlay(null)`. SVG scene drawing leaves the overlay group alone, and Canvas retains overlay state. A static chart can retain the old cursor or outline. `clear` also leaves `mode`, `source`, `pointer`, `dismissed` and `proxyFocused` available to a later full build. Clear the old overlay while its frame exists, reset input state, and hand focus to a surviving control or a programmatically focusable figure without adding a compact tab stop. **A2, C1, WCAG 2.4.3.** [interaction.ts:1161] [interaction.ts:1276] [interaction.ts:1292]\n\n7. **M · Keyboard zoom-in can zoom out. Ran.** On a line with x values `0,10,…,100`, set a supported public window `[40,45]`. The gesture minimum is 20. At center 42.5, the zoom-in calculation returned `[32.5,52.5]`. The toolbar disables Zoom in, but `+` and inward wheel still call `scaled`, which accepts any different result. Require a strictly narrower result for zoom-in and a strictly wider result for zoom-out. Apply the same decision to wheel consumption. **I3, I4.** [focus.ts:285] [interaction.ts:629]\n\n8. **M · Two unavailable plot states expose misleading slider behavior. Ran state and lookup inputs, reasoned through the DOM.** Put a zoomed chart into loading. I obtained `ready=false`, `zoom=true`, `zoomed=true`, `tooSmall=false` with the visible message `Loading chart.` The recovery branch therefore exposes a slider saying `No data to show.` Separately, hide both series on a three-x line. I obtained `ready=true`, native maximum 2, no first ref and no primary at ordinal 1. An adjustment snaps back to zero. Distinguish an actual empty zoom from loading/error, and represent a plot with no readable series accurately while keeping legend recovery available. **L7, A2, A3, WCAG 4.1.2.** [interaction.ts:285] [interaction.ts:413]\n\n9. **M · Some selectable data have no usable touch target. Reasoned.** Tap a narrow two-series plot, then tap the secondary readout row. The passive readout lets the tap through. Even in a floating tip, a clickable row is an `aria-hidden` plain `div` with no 24 px minimum and no spacing between adjacent choices. On donut, a zero or null slice has no pickable arc, its list item has no action, and keyboard Enter can select it. Provide accessible, touch-sized row or slice actions while preserving the two closed-chart tab stops through the existing composite controls. Close or suspend a pin when the table replaces the plot, including when the disclosure was activated by keyboard without a document `pointerdown`. **I4, A2, F8, WCAG 2.5.8 and 4.1.2.** [tooltip.ts:88] [figure.ts:126] [interaction.ts:1098] [css.ts:154]\n\n10. **M · Gesture work survives cancellation or a change of owner. Reasoned.** Start a pinch, move, then blur the window or receive `pointercancel` before the queued frame. The callback can still submit `pinchWindow`. During a two-finger pinch, adding finger C and lifting A also leaves B/C using the original A/B baseline. On a nonzoomable plot, a mouse press followed by release outside can leave `down` set because capture was conditional on zoom. Centralize cancellation, release ownership on every exit and cancel the pending frame on cancellation, detach and rebuild. Preserve the final valid move on an ordinary release. **I3, I4, plan 1.5.** [interaction.ts:783] [interaction.ts:849] [interaction.ts:918]\n\n11. **M · Bar swatches repeat an identical cue at series 33. Reasoned.** Render 33 default-color bar series and compare series 9 and 33. Both use palette slot one, the same square chip and the diagonal pattern. The plotted bars use the same three-pattern cycle. The ninth-series test cannot detect this collision. Extend the shared cue assignment so later cycles remain distinguishable in marks, legend and tooltip. **F9, A5.** [interaction.ts:113] [swatch.ts:133] [bars.ts:107]\n\n## 3. Tests and coverage\n\n**Tests that can pass with the named behavior broken.** These are reasoned findings from the assertions.\n\n| Test | What can still be broken when it passes |\n|---|---|\n| [focus2.test.ts:68] | The coarse-double loop breaks on `null` or an unchanged window before its first assertion. The supplied fixture does exactly that. Require a known representable narrowing, check finite ordered endpoints, then check exhaustion. |\n| [review2.browser.test.ts:277] and [the 40-move test] | The click test counts four selections without checking their identities, and skips its rectangle assertion when no floating tooltip exists. The sweep reads the `hover` callback, allows `ceil(10000 / plotWidth)` indices of error, and uses one small stride. Stale visible text, wrong selected rows and a faster sweep can pass. Assert actual readout text and selected refs with small, large and coalesced moves. |\n| [review2.browser.test.ts:295] and [the table bounds test] | A clipped fallback passes `isVisible()`. The table test measures vertical bounds once at the opening size. Neither checks hit testing, right-side controls, text zoom, or shrinking an already open table. |\n| [review2.browser.test.ts:322], [the native range test] and [the secondary-row test] | These prove one anchored mouse halving, two synthetic aligned value changes and a locator click on a floating row. Fixed-width pan, narrowed scatter adjustment, a gradual hover approach and secondary touch selection can all fail. |\n| [interaction.browser.test.ts:130], [donut legend], [zoom exclusions] and [pinch] | The donut overlay test checks only tooltip text. The legend accepts a lone value on a fixture where value and percentage coincide. The exclusion test creates only a bar. The pinch test measures an opted-in centered line pinch, never the opt-out, horizontal off-center anchor, cancellation or remaining-finger tap. |\n| [review2.browser.test.ts:502], [the AX test] and [the stream test] | The page observer samples the final `aria-live` attribute. The 24-stream observer watches initially present live regions and focuses no plot. Neither observes speech. The AX test checks genuine role, number and bounds, but reads the decisive point text from the DOM. |\n| [review2.browser.test.ts:582] and [the area marker test] | If every plotted SVG path is solid, the dash comparison skips itself. The area test uses line *swatches* as its oracle, so both swatch implementations can agree while disagreeing with the plotted markers. Require the expected renderer-specific marks and compare them directly. |\n| [review2.browser.test.ts:243] and [axe.browser.test.ts:92] | The listener test accounts for signal aborts on elements. It never opens a table, creates a pin, queues a pinch, or exercises detach/rebuild. The extra axe test labels two scans “paused” and “zoomed” without asserting Resume or the changed domain first. A broken transition can scan the preceding state again. |\n\n**Coverage by criterion.** This distinguishes exercising a feature from verifying its acceptance outcome. The fixture loop really does run axe on bar, area, scatter and donut. That does not exercise native gestures, spoken navigation or reflow on those types. The relevant fixture definitions and assertions are in [a11y/util.ts:28], [axe.browser.test.ts:55] and [keyboard.browser.test.ts:118].\n\n| Criterion | Coverage for bar, area, scatter and donut |\n|---|---|\n| **I1** | Area has content and swatch checks, scatter has a pinned-point case, and donut has a slice-content case. Missing across the four is a pointer sweep asserting the type-specific target, displayed rows, edge placement, fallback, Escape and frame deadline. Grouped and horizontal bar picking particularly need a DOM-level check. |\n| **I2** | The click/Enter/Space/pressed-state sequence is line-only. Area and scatter get a click during axe setup. The bar fixture has one series. Donut’s informational list is separately tested and I treat series toggling as inapplicable there. |\n| **I3** | No equivalent-window test across clicks, keys, drag and pinch for continuous bar, area or scatter. Category bar has a negative test. Donut’s ordinary toolbar is checked elsewhere, but the named zoom-exclusion test never creates a donut. |\n| **I4** | Scatter gets a real tap. No complete single-pointer, one-finger scroll and target-size exercise for any of the four, including tooltip rows and arcless donut slices. |\n| **I5** | No per-type first-frame/2.5-second pixel comparison, reduced-motion update step or 24-chart update timing. The line test samples settled CSS animation state. |\n| **I6** | Scatter exercises Pause/Resume and call order. The combined scatter test only requires the paused snapshot to be truthy before checking the resumed result. The frozen-view/control sequence is missing for bar, area and donut. |\n| **A1** | Fixture scans and rendering smoke tests exercise chart structure. They do not check the full accessible name and generated description against each type’s fixture, including ranges, extrema, last values, zone and omissions where applicable. |\n| **A2** | Bar checks End and donut checks focus on one zero slice. Area and scatter have pure navigation checks. None of the four gets the line chart’s complete forward/reverse tab and keyboard walk across states. |\n| **A3** | Bar has an AX role/ordinal assertion, area has generated stack text, and scatter has pure series steps. No full focused-text walk for the four types or recorded native touch-reader adjustment. All five required reader/browser rehearsals remain open. |\n| **A4** | Axe opens the table on all four and donut checks category rows. The full HTML cell, paging, jump, focus, resize and disclosure sequence is missing for bar, area, scatter and donut. |\n| **A5** | Area markers and bar patterns/seams get targeted tests. Those tests do not establish mark-to-swatch agreement in every mode, scatter ties, donut seams or high-count cue uniqueness. The author reports a Canvas seam failure. I did not reproduce browser pixels. |\n| **A6** | No complete 320 CSS px, 400% zoom and text-spacing exercise across the four types and three engines, including open table and readout. |\n| **A7** | No focused-and-unfocused streaming announcement exercise for any of the four. Scatter pin removal and line live-region mutation counts address different outcomes. |\n| **A8** | Basic applicable fixture states are exercised across all four. The additional paused, zoomed, narrow-readout and internal-error scans are line-only. Grouped/all-zero donut and continuous or stacked bar states also need explicit coverage. |\n| **A9** | One release-wide deliverable is required. The acceptance index checks named evidence, but the published WCAG success-criterion table, author obligations, limits and corresponding test-existence check are absent. |\n| **A10** | Foreign-page language assertions are line-based. No equivalent checks for bar, area, scatter or donut summaries, partly translated range text, missing values, table cells or legend text. |\n\n**Verification and contract impact.** I created, edited and deleted no files. I loaded the requested commit’s sources into memory and ran the two named focus suites with filesystem caches disabled. I did not run a browser, screen reader, build, typecheck or full test suite. The test-run tail was:\n\n```text\n Test Files  2 passed (2)\n      Tests  20 passed (20)\n   Start at  05:38:37\n   Duration  293ms (transform 64%, tests 19%, import 16%, worker 1%)\n```\n\nAt the reviewed commit, localized pan controls, jump-error feedback and plot instructions need `Messages` entries such as `panLeft`, `panRight`, `jumpNotFound` and `plotInstructions`. Those keys appeared in the shared checkout **after** `4809516`. The instructions should describe the shipped keys accurately: Up/Down change series and Enter selects. The `DatumRef` comment that categories do not slide also needs correction. Its existing shape can support the DOM remapping fix. For F8, I treated zero and null slices as individually listed even when positive slices are grouped into Other.\n\n**Areas examined and found sound**\n\n- **Toolbar availability and roving focus — reasoned.** Unavailable zoom buttons use `aria-disabled`, remain arrow-focusable, and guard activation. A populated toolbar retains exactly one `tabindex=\"0\"`, including when that item is unavailable.\n- **Ordinary teardown — reasoned.** The implementation uses separate abort lifetimes for the build, document, toolbar, table and tooltip rows. Explicit destroy aborts them and cancels its pending pinch frame.\n- **Wheel ownership — reasoned.** Unfocused, Ctrl-wheel and Meta-wheel paths return before cancellation. The wheel listener is explicitly non-passive.\n- **Table structure — reasoned.** The lazy table has a caption, scoped row and column headers, a named focusable scroll region, bounded pages and guarded pager endpoints.\n- **Ordinary keyboard dismissal — reasoned.** With keyboard ownership, successive Escapes close the tooltip, leave narrowed mode, then pass through. Moving to another target clears dismissal.\n- **Reader note and state channel — reasoned.** The omission note remains readable with `aria-live=\"off\"`. State announcements use a separate polite region with language support.\n- **Ordinary zoom and horizontal-bar projection — ran.** The focus tests passed for center and edge scaling and clamped pan. The upper-quarter horizontal-bar calculation preserved the value under the fingers.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
