{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'F9|A9' docs/02-product-spec.md && cat reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core5.fix.report.md | sed -n '/Interaction review/,/not done/p' | head -45 && cat reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/tests-opus.review.md | head -30 | cut -c1-595 && rg -n 'focus|surround' docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md\"","aggregated_output":"102:| F9 | Up to eight series, no two share a color. Beyond eight, each series still has a unique combination of color and dash or marker (for bars: color and fill pattern, which gives 32 distinct combinations; beyond 32 bar series combinations repeat), and AX130 is raised. | Tests with 6, 8 and 12 series | R7 |\n145:| A9 | A conformance table lists every applicable WCAG 2.2 AA success criterion, the test that covers it, and what the author must supply (a meaningful title or `labelledBy`, series names, units, the page's own structure). It also states the limits in section 3.8. | Published with each release; a CI check that every listed test exists | R11 |\n### 3. Interaction review\n| Item | What changed | Old bundle |\n|---|---|---|\n| new 9 | Choosable tooltip rows and every donut slice-list entry (zero and null slices included) are buttons named by their text, with `tabindex=-1`, so the closed chart still has two tab stops. Clicking a slice entry selects it; the press does not move focus. Opening the table by keyboard closes a pin (this already worked). | 2 of 3 tests fail on old; the pin test passes on old and stays as a guard |\n| part 1 item 3 | All-zero donut with `table: false` still lists its slices. Already worked. | passes on old (guard) |\n| part 1 item 9 | `minimumWidth` is now at most half the axis and at least one unit in the last place: two or three positions over [0,100] can be halved, and [1e16, 1e16+4] halves to a width of 2. | Node tests in `focus2.test.ts` failed before the fix |\n| part 1 item 11 | A pinned scatter point is dropped when its point has no drawn anchor. Otherwise the group is rebuilt by picking at that anchor, so new coincident points are counted. | both tests fail on old |\n| part 1 item 12 | `core/chart.ts` `measure()` now gives layout pixels rather than transformed viewport pixels. One conversion (`cssScale` in `tooltip.ts`) is used for picking, anchors, tooltip size and placement, and the readout. Ratios within 2% are treated as offsetWidth's integer rounding, not as a transform. | fails on old |\n| part 1 item 15 | An open table and its control stay inside the card when the card shrinks and text doubles. Already worked: no inline heights. | passes on old (guard) |\n| language watcher | Changing `lang` on the document changes number formatting to `1.234,5`, marks built-in text `lang=\"en\"`, and runs no theme stage. | passes on old (guard) |\n\nThe new tests are in `test/browser/chart/review5.browser.test.ts`.\n\n**24 px rows trade-off (decision for you):**\n- With 24 px rows everywhere, the hover tooltip grew about 12 px. At two plot edges it then no longer fit beside the mark, so the existing test \"at each of the four plot edges…\" fell back to the readout and failed.\n- What I did: rows are 24 px when the tooltip is pinned by a tap, in the fixed readout, and in the slice list. A mouse-hover tooltip keeps text-height rows, which are still buttons.\n- So WCAG 2.5.8 is met wherever rows are acted on by touch. It is not met for mouse clicks on hover rows. If you want 24 px rows there too, tell me and I will change the edge test's fixture instead.\n\n### 4. Tests that passed with the behaviour broken\n- Click test (`review2`): now asserts the floating tooltip's title and the exact selected x at each of the four positions.\n- Contrast dash test: no longer skips when every plotted SVG path is solid.\n- Donut legend: a second fixture where value and share differ, expecting \"3 (60%)\" and \"2 (40%)\".\n- Zoom exclusions: now covers category bar, line, area and donut, by buttons and the `+` key.\n- Coarse-double loop in `focus2` (pass 4).\n\nExpectations I changed because the requirement changed: \"two or three positions cannot be zoomed\" (focus2 and review2) became the attainable-minimum rule; \"the legend has no buttons\" became buttons outside the tab order; the note runs in `snapshot.test.ts` are now per run.\n\n### 5. Reader helper, note runs and AX143\n- The stand-in is gone; the call site imports `seriesNotDrawnSentence` from `../reader`.\n- `FigureModel.noteSegments` takes the reader's runs. A run is marked `en` only on a page in another language.\n- Node test: overriding only `seeTable` leaves \"Siehe Tabelle.\" unmarked; overriding only the sentence marks the built-in \"See table.\" `en`.\n- `figure.ts` no longer adds spaces between runs; the reader's runs carry their own.\n- AX143 now passes `chartType`, `xKind` (taken from the frame for scatter as well) and `horizontal` for bars. Node test: vertical category bars get the horizontal-bar hint, a category line does not.\n\n### 6. Mixed-sign totals\n`focus.ts` already takes the total text from the lookup's tooltip model and computes no net. A Node test checks a stack of +3 and −2: the tooltip total is \"3 and -2\" and the focused text contains \"Total 3 and -2\".\n\nNone of my tests depend on worker threads, so the switch to forked processes changes nothing for them.\n\n### Edits outside `src/core` and `src/dom`\n- `src/render/svg.ts`: the C4 change only.\n- `src/format/number.ts` and `src/format/index.ts`: share precision only.\n- `src/theme/css.ts`: the figure section (choice buttons).\n- Test files: `test/scale/domain.test.ts`, `test/format/index.test.ts`, `test/browser/render/review.browser.test.ts`.\n\n<!-- Reviewer: Codex gpt-6.2-astra, effort xhigh (observed in the session rollout, thread 01a1ed47-781f-7770-8b66-5525f5e0b457), read-only, cross-provider. Reviewed commits 13b2f63 and bf8fabf. -->\n\n**The release gate can pass with required behavior removed.** The motion tests disable animations while measuring them. The table tests accept values under the wrong column headers. The reference tests accept a chart with neither reference mark drawn. The distribution suite also passes declarations that fail in a NodeNext TypeScript consumer.\n\nI reviewed `13b2f63` and `bf8fabf`, and checked the five residual failures against `77213c0`. I ran read-only Node, TypeScript, esbuild and font-measurement probes. I did not launch a browser or change files. Browser results below are attributed to the recorded runs.\n\n### Findings\n\n**B** means the test can be cited for a criterion it does not establish. **M** means a major limitation. **m** means a narrower defect.\n\n1. **B · I5 · Reasoned. The screenshot helper suppresses the behavior under test.** Every motion screenshot passes `animations: 'disabled'`. Playwright fast-forwards finite CSS animations and transitions. A one-second entry fade or a transition under reduced motion can therefore produce equal screenshots. `createRaw` also waits for readiness and three frames before the “first” capture. The update test waits two frames, allows another 300 ms, and never requires the updated picture to differ from the old one. An ignored update passes. Capture with animations enabled from the first eligible \n\n2. **B · Packed TypeScript API · Ran. A supported ESM consumer loses the public types.** Both supplied Bundler-mode projects compile. Running the npm fixture with `--module NodeNext --moduleResolution NodeNext --noEmit` produces TS2834 at lines 5 and 6 of the shipped `index.d.ts`, missing public exports, and unused `@ts-expect-error` directives. The React fixture also loses its core option types. The `types` condition is correctly first and `public.d.ts` ships. The failure is the extensionless `./public` inside the ESM declaration. Emit `./public.js` in those declaration references and co\n\n3. **B · DX7/P6 · Ran. The size measurement is reproducible, but CI succeeds over budget.** I recovered the original tarball from the local npm cache, verified its SHA-512 against the committed size record, and independently bundled its production ESM bytes with esbuild `write:false`. Everything measured **283,389 minified bytes, 100,093 gzip bytes, 97.75 KiB**. Line-only measured **282,947 / 99,956 / 97.61 KiB**. CI calls `size` without `--check`. Even with `--check`, the script compares rounded KiB, so 40 KiB plus one byte passes. Compare integer bytes against `targetKiB * 1024` and mak\n\n4. **B · L2/A5 · Ran the colour math, reasoned about the browser assertions. The palette test measures CSS colours before painting.** `seriesColors` takes the first computed `color` per SVG slot and fills a separate one-pixel canvas with it. The background comes from a CSS variable. Making the marks nearly transparent, changing their actual fill, or covering them leaves those readings intact. L2 similarly walks ancestor background colours, misses a mark painted behind text, and can pass an empty axis-colour loop. The severity-1 Machado coefficients, sRGB decoding, linear-RGB multiplicatio\n\n5. **B · A5 · Ran the separator counterexample, reasoned about grayscale. Both perceptual thresholds can give false passes.** I fed `separated` a transition with 0.80 pixel of background coverage. It reported one transition and zero touching. Stacked bars have an additional geometry check. Grouped bars, areas and donuts lack that protection, and the four-slice test accepts three transitions, missing the closing seam. The 85% marker intersection-over-union threshold is invented. Identical shapes can produce different thresholded masks through luminance, subpixel alignment or nearby ink. Ch\n\n6. **B · L1/L8/A6 · Ran the text helper, reasoned about layout. A chart can discard labels and still pass.** Removing date or numeric ticks leaves fewer boxes to collide. Only the bar fixture compares a category count. The suite does not establish remedy order, three-line wrapping, the rotation limit, middle truncation with recoverable full text, AX143’s suggestion, or unchanged orientation. I confirmed that two boxes overlapping by 1.1 px are ignored when they have the same owner. Tooltip/readout text is excluded. L8 accepts a visible multi-line summary and does not bracket the 120×80 pl\n\n7. **B · I1 and pointer/tooltip cells · Reasoned. Late, obscuring or incomplete tooltips can pass.** Every probe settles before reading, so there is no input-to-paint p95 measurement. Scatter and donut supply `marks: []`, making their pointer occlusion assertion vacuous. Bars check only the top eight pixels. The hover test jumps into the tooltip and checks visibility without checking that the same datum stayed put. The narrow readout can drop all eight rows and retain `+2 more`. A scatter radius of 20 px can pass the tested inside offsets and 30 px miss. Exercise a slow pointer path, hit-\n\n8. **B · A1/A4 · Ran the text oracle, reasoned about the table. Correct words can be attached to the wrong thing.** Swapping North and South’s cells while retaining their headers passes `toContain` for each value. Missing scatter series and donut category/percentage headers can pass the header subset check. The range test omits zoomable bars, and the layout test uses two fixed-height charts instead of the specified 24. For A1, I passed every compact summary-fragment assertion with the overall range statement removed. With an author description, retaining only that text, the zone and the o\n\n9. **B · A2/A7 and keyboard evidence · Reasoned. The tests can keep reading the old proxy after focus has gone elsewhere.** `valueText` reads the selected DOM element’s `aria-valuetext`. It never requires that element to be focused or exposed as a range. The focus-indicator test compares the tooltip with datum marks rather than the indicator. The streaming test uses a growing window while focused, strips position and donut percentage in one comparison, and omits donut from the stricter check. A stale proxy, a covered ring, or lost focus at eviction can pass. Assert the active element, its\n\n10. **B · L7/A7/I6 · Reasoned. State changes can reach the reader while the checked surface stays quiet.** L7 verifies one nonempty polite mutation on loading→ready. An unrelated announcement qualifies. Error and internal-failure messages need only differ or be nonempty, with no equivalent bounds, tick or announcement checks. A7’s observer misses a newly inserted, already populated alert whose parent is outside a live region. I6 freezes only SVG scene markup and checks a final row count. While paused, the summary, lookup or open table could advance, or Resume could duplicate one buffered \n\n35:**One token differs from the first version.** The dark focus token is a near-white `#e3e8f0` (14.4:1 on #16181d), not `#79a8ff`. The refit made that change, and candidate B changes no token.\n36:- **Why the change.** The old focus blue sits at OKLab L 0.735, exactly where a dark-mode series blue belongs. With it kept, no dark set I found had a first blue above L 0.55. The exact search with the blue asked for at 0.62 ended at `#3970c2`, L 0.55 (`configs/dark-n2.json`, best within 300 s).\n38:- **Light focus.** `#1f5fcc` is unchanged; `keyboard.browser.test.ts` hardcodes it.\n63:- tighter dark margins to the focus and muted tokens;\n113:| 5 | nearest series color to focus / reference / muted | ≥ 0.10 | 0.116 (cyan) / 0.121 (green) / 0.111 (cyan) | 0.104 (green) / 0.107 (green) / 0.102 (pink) |\n152:  - focus distance 0.104 (green against `#e3e8f0`);\n162:The second review (part B, finding 1) showed that a single-ink focus indicator cannot work with these colors:\n166:A datum outline crossing a mark therefore fails WCAG 1.4.11 wherever it is drawn in one ink. The chart core is changing the indicator to two tones: a ring in the focus token, with a surround in the background color between the ring and the marks. That work is not in my files.\n170:1. **The focus color must reach 3:1 against the background.** The ring is seen against its surround, which is the background color. Light `#1f5fcc` on `#ffffff` is 5.89:1; dark `#e3e8f0` on `#16181d` is 14.43:1. Both pass, and the L2 token test asserts it.\n171:2. **The surround must reach 3:1 against every mark it touches.** That is constraint 1 read the other way: every series color reaches 3:1 on its background (lowest 3.10 light, 3.07 dark), and so do the axis and reference tokens. Nothing new is needed.\n172:3. **The focus color needs no contrast against series colors.** The surround keeps them from touching.\n174:**Constraint 5 is no longer needed for contrast.** That constraint is the 0.10 OKLab distance between every series color and the focus token, and it never could provide contrast: an OKLab distance is not a contrast ratio. What it buys is identity. The ring's color never coincides with a series color, so a focused datum's outline is not read as a mark of its own or another series. That matters most for slot 1, the most-used series. The old dark focus `#79a8ff` is 0.050 from today's dark blue `#5d9afd`, so a ring around a series-1 point would be drawn in nearly that series' own color. I recommend keeping constraint 5. Dropping it would allow `#79a8ff` to return (7.49:1 on the background, so it would pass under two tones) at the cost of that confusion. This is the owner's call.\n218:- **Tighter dark margins to the tokens:** focus 0.104 and muted 0.102. The superseded set had 0.138 and 0.113.\n296:3. **Dark against that light set.** The searches with the old focus token failed: `dark-a2.json` (annealing) and `dark-n2.json` (exact; best blue L 0.55). Moving the dark focus to near-white let annealing find a set with a light dark-mode blue and a deeper purple (`configs/dark-c.json`).\n326:- **Magenta fourth instead of purple**, to relieve the dark blue (`configs/mag4-a.json`): feasible on white. Its dark set was proved infeasible with the old focus token (exact search, `configs/dark-n2-m4.json`), and annealing found none with the new one (`configs/dark-b2-m4.json`).\n343:- **The dark focus ring is near-white `#e3e8f0`** (light stays blue).\n379:  - `keyboard.browser.test.ts` checks the light focus only, and that token is unchanged.\n382:- **The focus ring.** The two-tone focus ring is being built in the chart core. Its rendered contrast against marks has not been measured here.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
