ry value was left out (F12, C5): it explains the empty state.\n    const noteShown = shown.kind === 'ready' || shown.kind === 'empty' || shown.kind === 'error';\n    const modelFor = (reader: ReaderModel, tooSmall: boolean): FigureModel => {\n      const message = shown.kind === 'ready' ? null : shown.text;\n      // A compact chart is one image: its description says what state it is in (C1).\n      // Loading and error are not empty (documentation findings 1 and 3): their description is the state, not\n      // a reading of data the plot does not show.\n      const summary = message === null ? reader.summary\n        : shown.kind === 'empty' ? (compact ? `${message} ${reader.summary}` : reader.summary) : message;\n      return {\n        compact, title: named ? null : title, labelledBy: named ? by : null, name: reader.name || a.name,\n        subtitle: stringOption(a.options, 'subtitle'), summary,\n        summaryVisible: tooSmall, note: noteShown ? reader.note : null, message,\n        messageLang: message !== null && shown.kind !== 'ready' && shown.message ? english(shown.message) : null,\n        noteLang: noteLangFor(currentHidden, disclosureNow()),\n        // The reader's runs, each in its own language (reader review, finding 5); English is marked only on a\n        // page in another language (A10).\n        noteSegments: noteShown && reader.note !== null ? noteRuns(reader) : null,\n        // The generated summary's sentences are the built-in templates; the names in them do not change that.\n        summaryLang: message !== null && shown.kind !== 'ready' && shown.kind !== 'empty'\n          ? (shown.message && english(shown.message) ? 'en' : null)\n          : english('chartSummary') && english('seriesSummary') ? 'en' : null,\n      };\n    };\n    const zoomOn = zoomApplies(view, a.options.zoom) ? (a.options.zoom === 'touch' ? 'touch' as const : true) : false;\n    const zoomed = view.kind !== 'donut' && a.zoom !== null && (view.xDomain[0] > view.xFull[0] || view.xDomain[1] < view.xFull[1]);\n    const legend = a.options.legend === false ? false : (['top', 'right', 'bottom'] as const).find(p => p === a.options.legend) ?? 'bottom';\n    const controlsFor = (reader: ReaderModel, tooSmall: boolean): ControlsState => ({\n        const plot = figure.plot.getBoundingClientRect();\n        // Layout pixels, which the marks, the text and picking use: a CSS transform on an ancestor scales the\n        // bounding rect, not the layout (interaction review, part 1 item 12). Below 2% the ratio is the\n        // integer rounding of offsetWidth, and the fractional rect is kept.\n        const scaleOf = (rect: number, layout: number) => {\n          const k = layout > 0 && rect > 0 ? rect / layout : 1;\n          return Math.abs(k - 1) < 0.02 ? 1 : k;\n        };\n        const sx = scaleOf(plot.width, figure.plot.offsetWidth);\n        const sy = scaleOf(plot.height, figure.plot.offsetHeight);\n        // When the plot is squeezed to nothing, how far the rest overflows the figure is how much room is\n        // missing; that keeps the L8 decision continuous below zero.\n        const overflow = plot.height < 1 ? Math.max(0, figure.figure.scrollHeight - figure.figure.clientHeight) : 0;\n        const stage = figure.stage.getBoundingClientRect();\n        return { width: plot.width / sx, height: plot.height / sy - overflow, room: { width: stage.width / sx, height: stage.height / sy } };\n      },\n      devicePixelRatio: () => win.devicePixelRatio || 1,\n      // Scoped to this chart's own figure: properties and the scheme go with it (finding 10).\n      theme: (keys, colors) => resolveTheme(figure.figure, keys, colors),\n      surfaces(visible) {\n8:export function sameTarget(a: PickTarget | null, b: PickTarget | null): boolean {\n13:    return a.count === b.count && a.refs.length === b.refs.length && a.refs.every((r, i) => {\n194:    const r = record('donut', { title: 'D', data: [{ l: 'a', v: 1 }, { l: 'b', v: 2 }], label: 'l', value: 'v' });\n547:    const r = record('donut', { title: 'Z', data: [{ l: 'A', v: 1 }, { l: 'B', v: 2 }], label: 'l', value: 'v', onDiagnostic: (d: Diagnostic) => seen.push(d) });\npackages/axylo/test/browser/chart/review5.browser.test.ts:42:  it('tooltip rows pinned by a tap are buttons named by their text, at least 24 px tall, outside the tab order; a tap selects that series', async () => {\npackages/axylo/test/browser/chart/review5.browser.test.ts:67:  it('a zero and a null donut slice can be chosen in the slice list by pointer', async () => {\npackages/axylo/test/browser/chart/review5.browser.test.ts:85:  it('a pinned tooltip closes when the table replaces the plot, with the disclosure activated by keyboard', async () => {\npackages/axylo/test/browser/chart/review5.browser.test.ts:101:  it('an all-zero donut with table: false still lists its slices', async () => {\npackages/axylo/test/browser/chart/review5.browser.test.ts:125:  it('pinned (5, 100): changing the y domain to [0, 10] drops the pin', async () => {\npackages/axylo/test/browser/chart/review5.browser.test.ts:135:  it('a pin on a point that gains coincident neighbours shows the new count', async () => {\npackages/axylo/test/browser/chart/review5.browser.test.ts:152:  it('under scale(2) on an ancestor, hovering a bar picks that bar and the tooltip is placed beside it', async () => {\npackages/axylo/test/browser/chart/review5.browser.test.ts:188:  it('opened at 600 × 360, then the card shrinks and text grows: table and its control stay inside the figure', async () => {\npackages/axylo/test/browser/chart/review5.browser.test.ts:215:  it('changing lang on the document re-renders the language marking and the number formatting, without a theme callback', async () => {\npackages/axylo/test/browser/chart/review5.browser.test.ts:239:  it('A7 bar: a sliding category window does not rewrite the text of the focused, unchanged datum', async () => {\n5. **M · `keep` does not bound retained memory. Ran the aligned store. Reasoned through controller ownership.** After 1,000 appends with `keep: 3`, the frame held 3 rows and the store reported **1,001 retained rows**. Retiring superseded revisions reduced retention to 3. The controller never calls `retire`. Paused scatter and donut charts additionally keep a copy of every incoming batch until Resume, even with `keep: 1`. Retain the displayed snapshot and the latest accepted state, retire superseded revisions, and apply retention to the pending store as data arrives. Preserve rows still addressable from the displayed frame. **I6, plan 1.5, P7.\n\n6. **M · A clamped key can reverse the next assistive action. Reasoned.** Press Home at ordinal 0. The handler saves `intent = 'first'`, but the native value does not change, so no `input` clears it. An assistive increment to 1 then consumes the stale Home and returns to 0. Held keys at an endpoint have the same problem. A direct assistive value-set from 10 to 70 is interpreted as one page; a `change`-only notification has no handler. Also, tabbing onto a hovered plot sets `source = 'keyboard'` before `takeover` can adopt the pointer target. Scope key intent to its native action, reconcile the requested absolute ordinal once across supported \n\n7. **M · Keyboard exploration drops information available to the pointer. Ran.** With A at x=0 and 2 and hidden B at x=1, lookup counted three slots and the pointer reported “No observation” at 1. Right jumped directly from 0 to 2. For coincident scatter points, the pointer target contained two refs while the keyboard-created target contained one. On alternating scatter series, switching from A to B and pressing Right landed back on A. For a stacked area with values 2 and 3, the tooltip total was 5 and the all-series value text omitted it. On an incomplete stack, the text repeated “incomplete” per series and omitted the total. Use the lookup’\n\n8. **M · Panning and selection lack single-pointer equivalents. Reasoned.** Zoom in, then try to move the window right using taps or clicks alone. The toolbar only scales around the center or resets. Shift+Right, a drag, or a two-finger gesture can reach ranges that the buttons cannot. A mouse click and Enter/Space emit `select`; a touch tap only pins. A fresh mouse user also cannot explicitly select a secondary aligned series at the same x as the primary series. Provide single-click pan controls and a selectable datum or tooltip row that works with touch, mouse, and keyboard. **I3, I4, WCAG 2.5.1 and 2.5.7.** [interaction.ts:662](/home/canar\n\n9. **M · Enabled zoom controls can do nothing or produce a zero-width window. Ran.** For `[0,100]` with two or three aligned x positions, the computed minimum width was 200 or 100. Zoom in returned `null` at full view. Drag accepts half that minimum, so one input method can reach a range another cannot. On scatter `[10¹⁶,10¹⁶+4]`, the second halving produced two identical endpoints. The focused wheel calls `preventDefault()` even when zoom cannot move. Category line, area, and bar all returned `false` from `zoomApplies`, although §3.6 gives the category exclusion specifically to bars. Use one representable minimum across gestures, enforce str\n\n10. **M · Gesture cancellation and pinch anchoring are inconsistent. Reasoned; ran the horizontal projection arithmetic.** Start a captured drag and press Escape. The document handler clears `down` without releasing capture or consuming the active cancellation, so a containing dialog can close too. There is no lost-capture or window-blur cleanup. A second non-touch pointer overwrites the drag, and a remaining pinch finger can be treated as a fresh tap after the other lifts. On a horizontal continuous bar axis, the pinch calculation measures the fraction downward while the scale increases upward. In a `[0,100]` projection, halving at the upper\n\n11. **M · A pinned scatter tooltip survives removal of its marks. Ran the lookup distinction. Reasoned through commit.** Tap to pin a scatter point, then programmatically zoom it out of view. Commit keeps the old target if `lookup.tooltip(oldTarget)` returns any rows. That lookup can format a source row outside the current view. I also obtained two tooltip rows for two previously coincident points after hiding both series in a new lookup. Validate pinned refs using their visible ordinals, rebuild the coincident set and count for the displayed revision, and dismiss when the pinned datum leaves it. **I1, A7, `PickTarget`, plan 1.5.** [interacti\n\n12. **M · Tooltip positioning can cover a mark, escape the viewport, or be clipped. Reasoned.** Hover a chart under `transform: scale(2)`. Pointer deltas are viewport pixels passed to a layout-pixel lookup, and the tooltip translation mixes viewport bounds with unscaled `offsetWidth` and `offsetHeight`. In an overflow-clipped card, a candidate can fit the viewport but extend beyond the card. When no candidate fits, the last branch still shows a clamped rectangle, even across the active mark or when the tooltip is taller than the viewport. Scrolling a pinned or keyboard-focused chart does not recheck placement. Convert coordinates consistently\n\n13. **M · The narrow readout covers the controls and disappears when approached. Reasoned.** In the 260 px tile, hover a point and move onto the readout. `pointerleave` exempts only the floating tooltip, so the readout disappears. When touch-pinned, the opaque readout sits directly over the toolbar at `z-index: 3`; tapping it counts as inside and cannot activate the button underneath. Keyboard dismissal also persists too long: dismiss at A, move to B, then return to A, and A is still suppressed. Reserve a reachable readout area, include it in hover persistence, and clear dismissal on the first target change from either input source. **I1, I4,\n\n14. **M · Keyboard focus can be entirely invisible on a donut. Ran the overlay and layout. Reasoned about clipping.** Tab to a donut whose first slice has value zero and whose second slice is positive. In a 320×200 layout, the plot occupied `(0,0,320,200)`. The first slice had no anchor and produced no overlay. The remaining CSS focus outline is drawn outside that exact rectangle and clipped by the plot’s `overflow: hidden`; the native input is transparent. Draw the plot ring inside its bounds and visibly identify the focused legend entry for an arcless slice. **F8, A2, WCAG 2.4.7.** [focus.ts:240](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/\n\n15. **M · Opening the table overflows fixed cards and moves auto-height neighbors. Reasoned.** Open a table in a short fixed-height card. The code freezes the plot at its former pixel height, then inserts the table and pager below the controls. A clipping card hides them; an unclipped card can cover the next chart. In an auto-height stack, following charts move. A 100 px plot can also receive a 160 px scroll region because of `Math.max(160, height)`. The 24-chart test compares fixed outer tile rectangles, which remain equal even when their contents overlap. Allocate a bounded, responsive table area and test actual content bounds after opening\n\n16. **M · An open table announces incoming data. Reasoned.** Leave page 1 open with 200 rows and append row 201. Rendering changes the polite region from “Page 1 of 2” to “Page 1 of 3” without reader action. Page clamping after eviction can do the same. The 24-stream test opens no tables and observes only live regions present at setup. Keep the visible count current and announce page changes only from reader-initiated paging or jumping. **A7, WCAG 4.1.3.** [table.ts:64](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/table.ts:64) · [table.ts:132](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/table.ts:132) · [interaction.browser.test\n\n17. **M · One `tabindex=\"0\"` does not guarantee that focus survives a render. Reasoned.** Focus legend B, then update wide fields from `[A,B]` to `[B,A]`. The renderer moves B using `insertBefore`; ordinary removal and reinsertion can drop focus. Its restoration path runs only when the key disappears. With focus in the jump field, disabling the table removes the active element without a handoff. Shrinking to one page likewise hides the focused pager. Preserve or restore the active control after reordering, and move focus to the disclosure, scroll region, or another surviving control before removing a focused subtree. **A2, A4, WCAG 2.4.3.** [\n\n18. **M · “Jump to” can reject the displayed x or jump to another one. Ran the table model. Reasoned through the field.** The model accepted canonical ISO dates and instants, but rejected its own displayed `May 31, 2026` and `May 31, 2026, 12:00 AM UTC`. The field also rejects the displayed English `900,000`, while removing all whitespace turns `1 2` into 12. Its parser captures the x kind when the table opens, so changing a numeric axis to categories can coerce a valid category such as `002` into a different target. Invalid input only sets `aria-invalid`. Use the current model’s parser, preserve exact category labels, and provide an accepted\n\n19. **M · The plot ignores translation keys and exposes English under the page language. Ran the value formatter. Reasoned through language inheritance.** Overrides for `pointAll`, `seriesValue`, and `position` still produced `0: a 2; b 3. Point 1 of 2.` The role description uses `typeLine` directly instead of `plotRole`. On a German page, the default slider label, role description, value text, generated summary, and polite state announcement can all inherit German. The note’s language also depends on *all three* note keys being untranslated, regardless of which sentence is displayed. Apply the existing message templates and assign language w\n\n20. **M · An axis can retain the wrong unit after an update. Ran.** I built a wide line with A and B both in `ms`, then changed only B to `s`. The production view and layout-input paths produced `Value (ms)` and `Value`, respectively, but `sameKey` returned `true`. The controller can therefore keep a milliseconds axis title while the tooltip and table use the new units. Include the effective axis title and shared unit in the layout key. **L6, plan 1.5.** [axes.ts:100](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/axes.ts:100) · [axes.ts:165](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/axes.ts:165)\n5:The table that lists every applicable WCAG 2.2 level AA success criterion, the test that covers it and what the author must supply (criterion A9 of the specification) is produced separately from the acceptance tests' evidence, and published with each release. It has not been produced yet, and nothing on this page may be read as a claim of conformance.\n     1\t# Decision 0004: where the tooltip goes, and when the focused point's text changes\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: decided by the coordinator on 2026-11-18 after the second review of the interaction layer (`reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core3.interaction.review.md`, new findings 3 and 4). Not rehearsed with a screen reader or a screen magnifier; both parts need that before they are called verified.\n     4\t\n     5\t## 1. The tooltip is anchored to the datum and never takes the pointer (revision 2)\n     6\t\n     7\tRevision 1 (placed across the reading axis, offset from the pointer, interactive while hovered) was implemented three times and still failed the acceptance tests: because the tooltip's position followed the pointer it moved away when approached, because it took pointer events it stalled the readout or swallowed the click whenever a move landed on it, and it could cover controls and the focus ring. Revision 2 removes the causes instead of tuning distances.\n     8\t\n     9\t**Problem.** WCAG 1.4.13 requires content shown on hover to stay when the pointer moves onto it. Spec I1 requires the readout to name the datum at the pointer on every move, the tooltip not to cover the active mark, to stay inside the viewport, and to fall back to a fixed readout when it cannot fit. On a line chart the whole plot picks, so anything that takes pointer events inside the plot blocks picking.\n    10\t\n    11\t**Rules.**\n    12\t\n    13\t1. **Position depends on the datum only.** For the datum on show, the tooltip is placed against the bounding box of that datum's active marks (all series at that x for line, area and bar; the point for scatter; the slice for donut), enlarged by the focus ring's width. The pointer's own position never enters the computation, so the tooltip does not move while the datum is unchanged, and it cannot run away from an approaching pointer.\n    14\t2. **Candidates, in order:** above the box, below it, then to the right and to the left of it (for horizontal bars: right, left, above, below). Along the other axis it is centred on the box and shifted only as far as needed. A candidate is valid when the tooltip lies wholly inside the plot rectangle, inside the viewport and inside every clipping ancestor, and overlaps neither the box nor any control. The first valid candidate is used. For a donut the first candidate is the hole, when the tooltip fits inside it.\n    15\t3. **A hovered tooltip never takes pointer events.** It is `pointer-events: none`. The plot keeps receiving every move, so picking never stalls at any speed, and a click goes to the plot and selects the datum under the pointer. Moving the pointer onto the tooltip therefore cannot hide it: on line, area and bar the tooltip stays for as long as the pointer's position along the reading axis still picks the same datum, which a straight approach across the reading axis does; on scatter and donut, while the pointer is inside the tooltip's rectangle the datum is held (computed from coordinates in the plot's own move handler, not by letting the tooltip take the event).\n    16\t3b. **A rest holds the datum along the way to the tooltip.** Rule 3 is enough when the tooltip sits across the reading axis (above or below), because a straight approach does not change what is picked. It is not enough when the straight path from the datum to its tooltip crosses positions that pick something else: a tooltip beside a tall bar or stack, a donut's tooltip in the hole or outside the ring, a scatter point's tooltip. For those: once the pointer has rested on a datum for 250 ms (no move larger than 2 CSS px), that datum is held while the pointer stays inside the corridor between its resting position and the tooltip's rectangle (the convex hull of the point and the rectangle) and inside the rectangle itself. Leaving the corridor, or leaving the rectangle on any side but back into the corridor, resumes picking at once. A pointer that has not rested is never held, so a sweep across the plot never stalls, whatever its speed. Placements across the reading axis never use the corridor.\n    17\t4. **Rows are not controls while hovered.** A hovered tooltip is text. Clicking or tapping the plot pins the tooltip for that datum; a pinned tooltip takes pointer events, stays until Escape, a click elsewhere, or its datum leaves the displayed frame, and its rows are buttons of at least 24 × 24 CSS px (WCAG 2.5.8) that select a series. Keyboard focus shows the same tooltip by the same placement; it is never over the focus ring because the box includes the ring.\n    18\t5. **Readout mode is decided by layout, not by hover.** When the plot is narrower than 240 CSS px or shorter than 140 CSS px, the figure is in readout mode: a strip of two text lines is reserved under the plot in the layout, and the content that would be in the tooltip is written there as running text (\"x: series value; series value; +N more\"), wrapping to the two lines and ending in \"+N more\" when it does not fit. The strip is part of the figure's flow: it covers nothing and nothing covers it. In readout mode there is no floating tooltip. A pinned datum makes the strip's entries buttons of 24 px height.\n    19\t6. **When no candidate is valid outside readout mode** (a tooltip taller than the room around a tall stack, for example), rows are dropped from the end and counted in \"+N more\" until a candidate is valid; if none is with one row, the tooltip is placed inside the plot at the candidate that overlaps the box least. This is the only case in which the active mark may be partly covered, and the table still has every value.\n    20\t7. Escape dismisses a hovered or pinned tooltip without moving the pointer, until the pointer reaches a different datum.\n    21\t\n    22\tRejected: revision 1; an interactive hovered tooltip with a \"safe triangle\" (still ambiguous when the reading axis and the approach coincide); always using the readout (loses the association between the values and the mark on plots that have room).\n    23\t\n    24\tWhat \"can itself be hovered\" (spec I1) means under this decision, for the tests: the pointer can be moved straight from the datum onto the tooltip's rectangle, and the tooltip is still shown, for the same datum, when the pointer is inside it. It does not mean the tooltip is the hit-test target.\n    25\t\n    26\t## 2. The focused point's text changes only for a reason the reader would name\n    27\t\n    28\t**Problem.** The plot's keyboard proxy is a native range whose value text describes the focused point, ending with its position (\"Point 4 of 120\"). While data streams in, the count changes on every append, so the text of a focused control changes without the reader acting. Spec A7 forbids unrequested announcements; whether a given screen reader speaks such a change cannot be tested here.\n    29\t\n    30\t**Rule.** The range's bounds and numeric value are always kept true to the displayed frame, silently. Its value text is rewritten only when:\n    31\t\n    32\t1. the reader moves (a key, an assistive adjustment, a pointer or touch selection that takes focus),\n    33\t2. the focused datum's own values change (the same x and series now read differently),\n    34\t3. the focused datum leaves the frame (the text must then say where focus went), or\n    35\t4. the plot regains focus.\n    36\t\n    37\tAn append that only changes the count or the ordinal of an unchanged datum does not rewrite the text. The position in the text can therefore be out of date until the next of the four events; it is recomputed then.\n    38\t\n    39\tTo check with a screen reader when one is available (added to decision 0001's list): idle appends with the plot focused are silent; an eviction of the focused point is spoken once; an increment immediately after an append lands on the expected neighbour and reads the current count.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_51","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"git show a518cbb:packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a6-a10.browser.test.ts | sed -n '137,205p;207,250p' && sed -n '26,118p' packages/axylo/test/browser/chart/review6.browser.test.ts && rg -n 'clip|keyboard|pinch|area marker|sweep' reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/acceptance-browser.align2.report.md | head -20\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_51","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"git show a518cbb:packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a6-a10.browser.test.ts | sed -n '137,205p;207,250p' && sed -n '26,118p' packages/axylo/test/browser/chart/review6.browser.test.ts && rg -n 'clip|keyboard|pinch|area marker|sweep' reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/acceptance-browser.align2.report.md | head -20\"","aggregated_output":"        await settle(page);\n      }\n      expect(await page.evaluate(() => (window as unknown as { liveChanges: string[] }).liveChanges)).toEqual([]);\n      expect(await closePage(page)).toEqual([]);\n    });\n\n    it(`A7 ${type}: with the plot focused, a sliding window keeps focus on its datum: the value text is not rewritten while the datum stays (decision 0004), the numeric position follows it, eviction moves focus to the next datum once, and the next key reads the current count`, async () => {\n      const page = await devPage();\n      await create(page, type);\n      await page.focus('#before');\n      await page.keyboard.press('Tab');\n      await page.keyboard.press('ArrowRight');\n      await page.keyboard.press('ArrowRight');\n      const before = await readRange(page);\n      expect(before.focused && before.role === 'slider').toBe(true);\n      expect(before.value).toBe(2);\n      // The datum the reader is on, in its own words (series names, x, values), and the window size.\n      const own = (t: string) => t.replace(/\\.?\\s*Point \\d+ of \\d+\\.?$/, '').replace(/\\s*\\([^)]*%\\)/, '');\n      const size = before.max + 1;\n      await watchLive(page);\n      const keep = { line: 5, area: 5, bar: 4, scatter: 5, donut: 4 }[type];\n      // Two appends: the first two x values leave, the reader's datum stays, now at position 0.\n      for (let k = 0; k < 2; k++) await call(page, 'append', [streamRow(type, k), { keep }]);\n      const during = await readRange(page);\n      expect(during.focused).toBe(true);\n      expect(during.role).toBe('slider');\n      if (type === 'donut') expect(own(during.text)).toBe(own(before.text));\n      else expect(during.text, 'not rewritten for an unchanged datum').toBe(before.text);\n      expect(during.value, 'the numeric position follows the datum').toBe(0);\n      expect(during.max + 1).toBe(size);\n      // A third append evicts it: the text is rewritten once and names the datum focus went to.\n      await call(page, 'append', [streamRow(type, 2), { keep }]);\n      const after = await readRange(page);\n      expect(after.focused).toBe(true);\n      expect(own(after.text)).not.toBe(own(before.text));\n      // Where focus went is the lookup's rule; its text and its numeric position agree.\n      expect(after.text).toMatch(new RegExp(`\\\\b${after.value + 1} of ${after.max + 1}\\\\b`));\n      const evicted = after.text;\n      await call(page, 'append', [[], { keep }]);\n      expect((await readRange(page)).text).toBe(evicted);\n      // The next key lands on the neighbour and reads the current count.\n      await page.keyboard.press('ArrowRight');\n      const next = await readRange(page);\n      expect(next.value).toBe(after.value + 1);\n      expect(next.text).toMatch(new RegExp(`\\\\b${after.value + 2} of ${next.max + 1}\\\\b`));\n      expect(await page.evaluate(() => (window as unknown as { liveChanges: string[] }).liveChanges)).toEqual([]);\n      expect(await closePage(page)).toEqual([]);\n    });\n  }\n});\n\n// ── A8 ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\ntype Kind = ChartType | 'compact area' | 'compact bar';\ninterface State { name: string; enter(page: Page): Promise<void>; holds(page: Page): Promise<boolean> }\n\nconst button = (page: Page, label: string) => page.locator('#chart [role=\"toolbar\"] button', { hasText: label });\nconst visible = async (page: Page, sel: string) => (await page.locator(sel).count()) > 0 && page.locator(sel).first().isVisible();\n\n/** A place on a datum of the fixture, to point at: a scatter point, a donut slice, the middle of an aligned plot. */\nasync function onDatum(page: Page, type: Kind): Promise<{ x: number; y: number }> {\n  if (type === 'donut') { const g = await ring(page); return { x: g.cx + 0.85 * g.outer, y: g.cy }; }\n  if (type === 'scatter') return center((await shapes(page, '#chart', 14))[1]![1]!.box);\n  const p = await box(page, '#chart .axylo-proxy');\n  return { x: p.x + p.width / 2, y: p.y + p.height / 2 };\n}\n\n/** Every state a reader can bring a chart of `type` into, each with a check that it was really entered. */\nfunction states(type: Kind): State[] {\n  const list: State[] = [{ name: 'default', enter: async () => {}, holds: async page => visible(page, '#chart figure') }];\n  if (!compact) {\n    list.push({ name: 'keyboard focus with tooltip', enter: async page => { await page.focus('#before'); await page.keyboard.press('Tab'); await page.keyboard.press('ArrowRight'); }, holds: async page => (await readRange(page)).focused && visible(page, '#chart .axylo-tooltip') });\n    list.push({ name: 'pointer tooltip', enter: async page => { const p = await onDatum(page, type); await page.mouse.move(p.x, p.y); await settle(page); }, holds: async page => (await visible(page, '#chart .axylo-tooltip')) || visible(page, '#chart .axylo-readout') });\n    list.push({ name: 'table open', enter: async page => { await button(page, 'View as table').click(); await settle(page); }, holds: async page => visible(page, '#chart table') });\n    list.push({ name: 'streaming, paused', enter: async page => { await call(page, 'append', [streamRow(type as Full, 0)]); await button(page, 'Pause').click(); await settle(page); }, holds: async page => (await button(page, 'Resume').count()) === 1 });\n  }\n  if (type === 'line' || type === 'area' || type === 'scatter') {\n    list.push({ name: 'zoomed', enter: async page => call(page, 'zoom', [type === 'scatter' ? [12, 30] : ['2026-03-03', '2026-03-05']]), holds: async page => (await button(page, 'Reset zoom').count()) === 1 });\n  }\n  if (type === 'line' || type === 'area' || type === 'bar' || type === 'scatter') {\n    list.push({ name: 'a series hidden', enter: async page => { await page.locator('#chart .axylo-legend-entry').first().click(); await settle(page); }, holds: async page => (await page.locator('#chart .axylo-legend-entry').first().getAttribute('aria-pressed')) === 'false' });\n  }\n  list.push({ name: 'loading', enter: async page => call(page, 'update', [{ state: 'loading' }]), holds: async page => visible(page, '#chart .axylo-message') });\n  list.push({ name: 'empty', enter: async page => call(page, 'update', [{ data: [] }]), holds: async page => visible(page, '#chart .axylo-message') });\n  list.push({ name: 'error', enter: async page => call(page, 'update', [{ state: { error: 'The query failed.' } }]), holds: async page => visible(page, '#chart .axylo-message') });\n  return list;\n}\n\n/** A page with landmarks and a page heading, so a scan of the whole document judges the chart, not the test page. */\nasync function landmarkPage(): Promise<Page> {\n  const page = await devPage();\n  await page.evaluate(() => {\n    const main = document.createElement('main');\n    const h1 = document.createElement('h1');\n    h1.textContent = 'Dashboard';\n    // The chart's default heading is level 3, so the page gives it a level-2 section to sit in.\n    const h2 = document.createElement('h2');\n    h2.textContent = 'Energy';\n    main.append(h1, h2, document.querySelector('#before')!, document.querySelector('#chart')!, document.querySelector('#after')!);\n    document.body.appendChild(main);\n  });\n  return page;\n}\n\nasync function make(page: Page, type: Kind, variant: 'default' | 'dirty' = 'default'): Promise<void> {\n  if (type === 'compact area' || type === 'compact bar') {\n    const fn = type === 'compact area' ? 'area' : 'bar';\n    await createRaw(page, fn, { compact: true, title: 'Compact', data: variant === 'dirty' ? [{ d: 'a', v: 1 }, { d: 'b', v: '__NaN__' }, { d: 'c', v: 3 }] : [{ d: 'a', v: 1 }, { d: 'b', v: 2 }, { d: 'c', v: 3 }], x: 'd', y: 'v' });\n  } else await create(page, type, {}, '#chart', variant);\n}\n\ndescribe('A8 axe-core', () => {\n  for (const type of [...TYPES, 'compact area', 'compact bar'] as Kind[]) {\n  return page.evaluate(() => {\n    const t = document.querySelector('#chart .axylo-tooltip') as HTMLElement;\n    if (t.hidden) return { title: null, box: null };\n    const b = t.getBoundingClientRect();\n    return { title: t.querySelector('.axylo-tooltip-title')?.textContent ?? null, box: { x: b.x, y: b.y, width: b.width, height: b.height } };\n  });\n}\n\n/** A point 2 px inside `box`, nearest to `from`. */\nconst nearestInside = (box: Box, from: { x: number; y: number }) => ({\n  x: Math.min(box.x + box.width - 2, Math.max(box.x + 2, from.x)), y: Math.min(box.y + box.height - 2, Math.max(box.y + 2, from.y)),\n});\n\n/** Moves in `steps` straight steps, one frame each; returns the tooltip seen after each step. */\nasync function walk(page: Page, from: { x: number; y: number }, to: { x: number; y: number }, steps: number) {\n  const seen: { title: string | null; box: Box | null }[] = [];\n  for (let k = 1; k <= steps; k++) {\n    await page.mouse.move(from.x + (to.x - from.x) * k / steps, from.y + (to.y - from.y) * k / steps);\n    await frames(page);\n    seen.push(await tip(page));\n  }\n  return seen;\n}\n\n/** Tall bars, so a bar's tooltip has no room above or below and goes beside it. */\nasync function tallBars(page: Page): Promise<{ at: { x: number; y: number } }> {\n  await create(page, 'bar', { title: 'Tall', data: ['Q1', 'Q2', 'Q3', 'Q4', 'Q5', 'Q6'].map((x, i) => ({ x, y: [96, 100, 97, 99, 98, 95][i] })), x: 'x', y: 'y' });\n  const p = (await page.locator('#chart .axylo-proxy').boundingBox())!;\n  return { at: { x: p.x + p.width * (2.5 / 6), y: p.y + p.height * 0.6 } };\n}\n\ndescribe('rule 3b: a rest holds the datum along the corridor', () => {\n  it('bar: after a rest, a slow straight approach to the tooltip beside the bar keeps Q3, unmoved, and ends inside it', async () => {\n    const page = await chartPage({ css: '#chart { width: 640px; height: 360px; }' });\n    const { at } = await tallBars(page);\n    await page.mouse.move(at.x, at.y);\n    await frames(page, 2);\n    const start = await tip(page);\n    expect(start.title).toBe('Q3');\n    await page.waitForTimeout(320);\n    const target = nearestInside(start.box!, at);\n    const seen = await walk(page, at, target, 20);\n    for (const [k, s] of seen.entries()) {\n      expect(s.title, `step ${k + 1}`).toBe('Q3');\n      expect(s.box, `step ${k + 1}`).toEqual(start.box);\n    }\n    expect(await closePage(page)).toEqual([]);\n  });\n\n  it('bar: without a rest, the same path changes the datum at the next band', async () => {\n    const page = await chartPage({ css: '#chart { width: 640px; height: 360px; }' });\n    const { at } = await tallBars(page);\n    await page.mouse.move(at.x, at.y);\n    await frames(page, 2);\n    const start = await tip(page);\n    const seen = await walk(page, at, nearestInside(start.box!, at), 20);\n    expect(seen.map(s => s.title).some(t => t !== 'Q3')).toBe(true);\n    expect(await closePage(page)).toEqual([]);\n  });\n\n  it('bar: leaving the rectangle away from the corridor resumes picking on that move', async () => {\n    const page = await chartPage({ css: '#chart { width: 640px; height: 360px; }' });\n    const { at } = await tallBars(page);\n    await page.mouse.move(at.x, at.y);\n    await frames(page, 2);\n    const start = await tip(page);\n    await page.waitForTimeout(320);\n    const target = nearestInside(start.box!, at);\n    await walk(page, at, target, 10);\n    expect((await tip(page)).title).toBe('Q3');\n    // Out of the rectangle downwards, away from the corridor, in one move: what is under the pointer there.\n    await page.mouse.move(target.x, start.box!.y + start.box!.height + 40);\n    await frames(page);\n    expect((await tip(page)).title).not.toBe('Q3');\n    expect(await closePage(page)).toEqual([]);\n  });\n\n  it('bar: a fast sweep through the tooltip\\'s rectangle after a rest elsewhere is never held', async () => {\n    const page = await chartPage({ css: '#chart { width: 640px; height: 360px; }' });\n    const { at } = await tallBars(page);\n    await page.mouse.move(at.x, at.y);\n    await frames(page, 2);\n    const start = await tip(page);\n    await page.waitForTimeout(320);\n    // Away, out of the corridor at once, then a sweep through where Q3's tooltip was.\n    const p = (await page.locator('#chart .axylo-proxy').boundingBox())!;\n    await page.mouse.move(p.x + 4, p.y + p.height - 4);\n    await frames(page);\n    const r = start.box!;\n    const seen = await walk(page, { x: r.x - 10, y: r.y + r.height / 2 }, { x: r.x + r.width + 10, y: r.y + r.height / 2 }, 12);\n    // What is under the pointer is shown: never Q3, which the pointer rested on before leaving its corridor.\n    expect(seen.map(s => s.title).filter(t => t === 'Q3')).toEqual([]);\n    expect(seen.some(s => s.title !== null)).toBe(true);\n24:  - **I1 bar, fast sweep:** one move per band, across a beside-placed tooltip, must show Q1 to Q6 in turn.\n25:  - **I1 donut, fast sweep:** one move per slice around the ring must show every slice in turn.\n43:The two readout-height tests now pass on both sides of the limit. So do the three bar converse tests and the donut sweep, since a pointer that has not rested is not held now either. L4 on a `prefers-color-scheme` change also passed on this build. Your official run on a quiet tree is still to come.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_52","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'pageForX|parseTime|parseWall|Date.parse' packages/axylo/src/reader/table.ts && rg -n 'ChartHandle|on\\\\(event|select' packages/axylo/src/public.ts | head -32 && sed -n '14,40p' reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core2.review.md | sed -E 's@ · \\\\[.*@@' | cut -c1-930\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_52","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n 'pageForX|parseTime|parseWall|Date.parse' packages/axylo/src/reader/table.ts && rg -n 'ChartHandle|on\\\\(event|select' packages/axylo/src/public.ts | head -32 && sed -n '14,40p' reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core2.review.md | sed -E 's@ · \\\\[.*@@' | cut -c1-930\"","aggregated_output":"115:    pageForX(x) { return page(readPosition(x, 'category', null, frame.labels), rowCount); },\n137:    pageForX(x) {\n157:    pageForX(x) {\n138:  select: Point<Row>;\n\n### Major\n\n5. **M · `keep` does not bound retained memory. Ran the aligned store. Reasoned through controller ownership.** After 1,000 appends with `keep: 3`, the frame held 3 rows and the store reported **1,001 retained rows**. Retiring superseded revisions reduced retention to 3. The controller never calls `retire`. Paused scatter and donut charts additionally keep a copy of every incoming batch until Resume, even with `keep: 1`. Retain the displayed snapshot and the latest accepted state, retire superseded revisions, and apply retention to the pending store as data arrives. Preserve rows still addressable from the displayed frame. **I6, plan 1.5, P7.** [chart.ts:358](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/chart.ts:358)\n\n6. **M · A clamped key can reverse the next assistive action. Reasoned.** Press Home at ordinal 0. The handler saves `intent = 'first'`, but the native value does not change, so no `input` clears it. An assistive increment to 1 then consumes the stale Home and returns to 0. Held keys at an endpoint have the same problem. A direct assistive value-set from 10 to 70 is interpreted as one page; a `change`-only notification has no handler. Also, tabbing onto a hovered plot sets `source = 'keyboard'` before `takeover` can adopt the pointer target. Scope key intent to its native action, reconcile the requested absolute ordinal once across supported event paths, and take over the pointer datum before changing source. **Decision 0001, A2, A3, WCAG 4.1.2.** [interaction.ts:432](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:432)\n\n7. **M · Keyboard exploration drops information available to the pointer. Ran.** With A at x=0 and 2 and hidden B at x=1, lookup counted three slots and the pointer reported “No observation” at 1. Right jumped directly from 0 to 2. For coincident scatter points, the pointer target contained two refs while the keyboard-created target contained one. On alternating scatter series, switching from A to B and pressing Right landed back on A. For a stacked area with values 2 and 3, the tooltip total was 5 and the all-series value text omitted it. On an incomplete stack, the text repeated “incomplete” per series and omitted the total. Use the lookup’s visible ordinal order and tooltip model for all-series navigation and text. Preserve the chosen series in narrowed mode. **Decision 0001, A2, A3, I1, §3.6.** [focus.ts:23](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/focus.ts:23)\n\n8. **M · Panning and selection lack single-pointer equivalents. Reasoned.** Zoom in, then try to move the window right using taps or clicks alone. The toolbar only scales around the center or resets. Shift+Right, a drag, or a two-finger gesture can reach ranges that the buttons cannot. A mouse click and Enter/Space emit `select`; a touch tap only pins. A fresh mouse user also cannot explicitly select a secondary aligned series at the same x as the primary series. Provide single-click pan controls and a selectable datum or tooltip row that works with touch, mouse, and keyboard. **I3, I4, WCAG 2.5.1 and 2.5.7.** [interaction.ts:662](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:662)\n\n9. **M · Enabled zoom controls can do nothing or produce a zero-width window. Ran.** For `[0,100]` with two or three aligned x positions, the computed minimum width was 200 or 100. Zoom in returned `null` at full view. Drag accepts half that minimum, so one input method can reach a range another cannot. On scatter `[10¹⁶,10¹⁶+4]`, the second halving produced two identical endpoints. The focused wheel calls `preventDefault()` even when zoom cannot move. Category line, area, and bar all returned `false` from `zoomApplies`, although §3.6 gives the category exclusion specifically to bars. Use one representable minimum across gestures, enforce strictly ordered finite endpoints, disable exhausted controls, and consume the wheel only for an effective change. Implement ordinal zoom for supported category charts. **I3, I4, §3.6.** [focus.ts:282](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/focus.ts:282)\n\n10. **M · Gesture cancellation and pinch anchoring are inconsistent. Reasoned; ran the horizontal projection arithmetic.** Start a captured drag and press Escape. The document handler clears `down` without releasing capture or consuming the active cancellation, so a containing dialog can close too. There is no lost-capture or window-blur cleanup. A second non-touch pointer overwrites the drag, and a remaining pinch finger can be treated as a fresh tap after the other lifts. On a horizontal continuous bar axis, the pinch calculation measures the fraction downward while the scale increases upward. In a `[0,100]` projection, halving at the upper quarter moved the value under the fingers from 75 to 87.5. Give each gesture explicit pointer ownership, cancel and release it on every exit, suppress post-pinch taps, and derive the anchor fraction from the projection. **I3, I4, decision 0001.** [interaction.ts:609](/home/canar\n\n11. **M · A pinned scatter tooltip survives removal of its marks. Ran the lookup distinction. Reasoned through commit.** Tap to pin a scatter point, then programmatically zoom it out of view. Commit keeps the old target if `lookup.tooltip(oldTarget)` returns any rows. That lookup can format a source row outside the current view. I also obtained two tooltip rows for two previously coincident points after hiding both series in a new lookup. Validate pinned refs using their visible ordinals, rebuild the coincident set and count for the displayed revision, and dismiss when the pinned datum leaves it. **I1, A7, `PickTarget`, plan 1.5.** [interaction.ts:977](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:977)\n\n12. **M · Tooltip positioning can cover a mark, escape the viewport, or be clipped. Reasoned.** Hover a chart under `transform: scale(2)`. Pointer deltas are viewport pixels passed to a layout-pixel lookup, and the tooltip translation mixes viewport bounds with unscaled `offsetWidth` and `offsetHeight`. In an overflow-clipped card, a candidate can fit the viewport but extend beyond the card. When no candidate fits, the last branch still shows a clamped rectangle, even across the active mark or when the tooltip is taller than the viewport. Scrolling a pinned or keyboard-focused chart does not recheck placement. Convert coordinates consistently, intersect clipping bounds, exclude the mark and focus ring, and use the readout when no candidate fits. **I1, A2, A6, WCAG 2.4.11.** [interaction.ts:296](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:296)\n\n13. **M · The narrow readout covers the controls and disappears when approached. Reasoned.** In the 260 px tile, hover a point and move onto the readout. `pointerleave` exempts only the floating tooltip, so the readout disappears. When touch-pinned, the opaque readout sits directly over the toolbar at `z-index: 3`; tapping it counts as inside and cannot activate the button underneath. Keyboard dismissal also persists too long: dismiss at A, move to B, then return to A, and A is still suppressed. Reserve a reachable readout area, include it in hover persistence, and clear dismissal on the first target change from either input source. **I1, I4, WCAG 1.4.13 and 2.4.11.** [interaction.ts:534](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:534)\n\n14. **M · Keyboard focus can be entirely invisible on a donut. Ran the overlay and layout. Reasoned about clipping.** Tab to a donut whose first slice has value zero and whose second slice is positive. In a 320×200 layout, the plot occupied `(0,0,320,200)`. The first slice had no anchor and produced no overlay. The remaining CSS focus outline is drawn outside that exact rectangle and clipped by the plot’s `overflow: hidden`; the native input is transparent. Draw the plot ring inside its bounds and visibly identify the focused legend entry for an arcless slice. **F8, A2, WCAG 2.4.7.** [focus.ts:240](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/core/focus.ts:240)\n\n15. **M · Opening the table overflows fixed cards and moves auto-height neighbors. Reasoned.** Open a table in a short fixed-height card. The code freezes the plot at its former pixel height, then inserts the table and pager below the controls. A clipping card hides them; an unclipped card can cover the next chart. In an auto-height stack, following charts move. A 100 px plot can also receive a 160 px scroll region because of `Math.max(160, height)`. The 24-chart test compares fixed outer tile rectangles, which remain equal even when their contents overlap. Allocate a bounded, responsive table area and test actual content bounds after opening, resizing, and text zoom. **A4, A6, WCAG 1.4.4, 1.4.10 and 2.4.11.** [interaction.ts:786](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/interaction.ts:786)\n\n16. **M · An open table announces incoming data. Reasoned.** Leave page 1 open with 200 rows and append row 201. Rendering changes the polite region from “Page 1 of 2” to “Page 1 of 3” without reader action. Page clamping after eviction can do the same. The 24-stream test opens no tables and observes only live regions present at setup. Keep the visible count current and announce page changes only from reader-initiated paging or jumping. **A7, WCAG 4.1.3.** [table.ts:64](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/dom/table.ts:64)\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
