{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_55","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba packages/axylo/src/reader/table.ts | sed -n '18,83p' && rg -n 'Point|series|positions|eight|eight' docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md | head -17 && nl -ba packages/axylo/test/browser/chart/review2.browser.test.ts | sed -n '308,337p;651,710p' && git diff --name-only a518cbb -- packages/axylo/src packages/axylo/test && rg -n 'pin|readout|release|own' docs/decisions/0001-plot-interaction-pattern.md\"","aggregated_output":"    18\t  return ordinal >= 0 && ordinal < count ? Math.floor(ordinal / PAGE_SIZE) : null;\n    19\t}\n    20\t\n    21\texport interface ReaderLabels {\n    22\t  readonly category: string;\n    23\t  readonly percentage: string;\n    24\t  readonly series: string;\n    25\t  readonly value: string;\n    26\t  /** Supplied with the labels because the aligned frame has no x unit. */\n    27\t  readonly xUnit?: string | null;\n    28\t}\n    29\t\n    30\ttype ScatterRow = PointIndex & { readonly missing?: true };\n    31\t\n    32\tfunction scatterRows(view: ScatterView, work?: PointOrderWork) {\n    33\t  const { frame, visiblePoints, xDomain: [min, max] } = view;\n    34\t  const points = pointOrder(frame.series, visiblePoints, work);\n    35\t  const gaps: (PointSeries & { readonly series: number })[] = [];\n    36\t  const ranges: IndexRange[] = [];\n    37\t  frame.series.forEach((series, s) => {\n    38\t    const x = series.missingX;\n    39\t    if (!x?.length) return;\n    40\t    const start = lowerBound(x, min);\n    41\t    const end = upperBound(x, max, start);\n    42\t    if (start === end) return;\n    43\t    // Within the missing rows, the shared x column leaves ties in series order.\n    44\t    gaps.push({ key: series.key, label: series.label, drawable: false, row: null, series: s, x, y: x });\n    45\t    ranges.push({ start, end });\n    46\t  });\n    47\t  if (!gaps.length) return points;\n    48\t\n    49\t  const missing = pointOrder(gaps, ranges, work);\n    50\t  const pointX = ({ series, index }: PointIndex) => frame.series[series]!.x[index]!;\n    51\t\n    52\t  function cursor(ordinal: number): () => ScatterRow | null {\n    53\t    // Find how many points precede this row without merging the earlier pages.\n    54\t    // The table keeps point order and puts missing observations after points at a tied x.\n    55\t    let low = Math.max(0, ordinal - missing.count);\n    56\t    let high = Math.min(ordinal, points.count);\n    57\t    while (low < high) {\n    58\t      const mid = low + Math.floor((high - low) / 2);\n    59\t      if (mid + missing.beforeX(pointX(points.at(mid)!)) < ordinal) low = mid + 1;\n    60\t      else high = mid;\n    61\t    }\n    62\t    const nextPoint = points.cursor(low);\n    63\t    const nextGap = missing.cursor(ordinal - low);\n    64\t    let point = nextPoint?.() ?? null;\n    65\t    let gap = nextGap?.() ?? null;\n    66\t    return () => {\n    67\t      if (gap && (!point || gaps[gap.series]!.x[gap.index]! < pointX(point))) {\n    68\t        const row: ScatterRow = { series: gaps[gap.series]!.series, index: gap.index, missing: true };\n    69\t        gap = nextGap?.() ?? null;\n    70\t        return row;\n    71\t      }\n    72\t      const row = point;\n    73\t      point = nextPoint?.() ?? null;\n    74\t      return row;\n    75\t    };\n    76\t  }\n    77\t\n    78\t  return { count: points.count + missing.count, cursor, beforeX: (x: number) => points.beforeX(x) + missing.beforeX(x) };\n    79\t}\n    80\t\n    81\t/** A chart kind's table, in buildTable's terms (a kind's composition holds only its own). */\n    82\texport type TableBuilder = (\n    83\t  view: View, caption: string, formatters: Formatters, messages: Messages, labels: ReaderLabels, locale?: string, work?: PointOrderWork,\n25:  series: 'channel',\n35:| Plain options object, not a builder or a JSX tree | The same object works in the React adapter. Recharts needed eight components for the trend task; the information content was four fields. |\n41:// long rows: one row per (x, series)\n42:line(el, { data: rows, x: 'day', y: 'sessions', series: 'channel' });\n44:// wide rows: one column per series\n51:Long and wide are mutually exclusive in the types: `series` cannot be combined with an array of `y`.\n101:| `colors` | A fixed color per series name (DX6). |\n105:There is no `width`, `height`, `responsive`, `theme`, `palette` or `animation` option. Size comes from the container, colors from CSS, motion from the spec (I5). An unknown key raises AX150 with the closest known name.\n156:<Line data={rows} x=\"day\" y=\"sessions\" series=\"channel\" title=\"Daily sessions by channel\" />\n186:| view model | Scales, visible index ranges, series visibility | Domain rules implement F1 and F7. |\n199:| view model | Frame revision, zoom, series visibility, author domains. |\n210:**This is reopened and decided in M1 by measurement.** The first draft justified canvas by Phase 1's slow SVG libraries, but those create a node per point; the one-path-per-series SVG chart in the harness was fast. With drawing simplified to a few vertices per pixel column (F5), an SVG path per series is small. SVG follows CSS variables and class-toggled themes with no repaint code, adapts to forced colors, prints sharply, needs no pixel-ratio handling and allocates no backing store (a 1,920×1,080 canvas at pixel ratio 2 is about 33 MB). Canvas is likely cheaper for per-frame redraw when streaming and hovering.\n224:    <p id=\"ax7-s\" class=\"axylo-summary\">Line chart, 3 series, …</p>\n305:- Tooltip tests at the edges, with sparse series, and in a narrow tile.\n   308\t    expect(await closePage(page)).toEqual([]);\n   309\t  });\n   310\t\n   311\t  it('in a card that clips its content, a tooltip that would be cut off is shown in the readout instead', async () => {\n   312\t    const page = await chartPage({ css: '#card { width: 360px; height: 260px; overflow: hidden; margin-top: 300px; } #card #chart { width: 360px; height: 260px; }' });\n   313\t    await page.evaluate(() => {\n   314\t      const card = Object.assign(document.createElement('div'), { id: 'card' });\n   315\t      const chart = document.getElementById('chart')!;\n   316\t      chart.replaceWith(card);\n   317\t      card.appendChild(chart);\n   318\t    });\n   319\t    const data = ['A', 'B', 'C', 'D', 'E', 'F', 'G', 'H'].flatMap(s => [0, 1, 2].map(x => ({ x, s, y: x + s.charCodeAt(0) })));\n   320\t    await create(page, 'line', { title: 'Clipped', data, x: 'x', y: 'y', series: 's' });\n   321\t    const box = (await page.locator('#chart .axylo-proxy').boundingBox())!;\n   322\t    await page.mouse.move(box.x + box.width / 2, box.y + box.height / 2);\n   323\t    const card = (await page.locator('#card').boundingBox())!;\n   324\t    const tip = await page.locator('#chart .axylo-tooltip').boundingBox();\n   325\t    if (tip) {\n   326\t      expect(tip.x).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(card.x);\n   327\t      expect(tip.y).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(card.y);\n   328\t      expect(tip.x + tip.width).toBeLessThanOrEqual(card.x + card.width + 0.5);\n   329\t      expect(tip.y + tip.height).toBeLessThanOrEqual(card.y + card.height + 0.5);\n   330\t    } else {\n   331\t      expect(await page.locator('#chart .axylo-readout').isVisible()).toBe(true);\n   332\t    }\n   333\t    expect(await closePage(page)).toEqual([]);\n   334\t  });\n   335\t});\n   336\t\n   337\tdescribe('single-pointer equivalents (WCAG 2.5.1, 2.5.7; finding 8)', () => {\n   651\tdescribe('the pointer readout follows every move (spec I1; the benchmark\\'s hover workload)', () => {\n   652\t  it('one series of 10,000 points, 40 evenly spaced real moves, one per frame: each names the datum nearest the pointer\\'s x', async () => {\n   653\t    const page = await chartPage();\n   654\t    const n = 10_000;\n   655\t    await page.evaluate(async n => {\n   656\t      const x = Float64Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => i);\n   657\t      const y = Float64Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => Math.sin(i / 50) * 100);\n   658\t      const chart = window.Axylo.line!('#chart', { title: 'Hover', data: { columns: { x, y } }, x: 'x', y: 'y' });\n   659\t      window.charts.push(chart);\n   660\t      (window as unknown as { hovered: number }).hovered = NaN;\n   661\t      chart.on('hover', p => { (window as unknown as { hovered: number }).hovered = p ? (p as { index: number }).index : NaN; });\n   662\t      await chart.__bench.ready();\n   663\t    }, n);\n   664\t    const box = (await page.locator('#chart .axylo-proxy').boundingBox())!;\n   665\t    const { xDomain } = await page.evaluate(() => window.charts[0]!.__bench.describe());\n   666\t    const y = box.y + box.height / 2;\n   667\t    const picked: number[] = [];\n   668\t    const expected: number[] = [];\n   669\t    for (let k = 0; k < 40; k++) {\n   670\t      const px = box.x + box.width * (0.1 + 0.8 * k / 39);\n   671\t      await page.mouse.move(px, y);\n   672\t      await page.evaluate(() => new Promise(r => requestAnimationFrame(() => r(null))));\n   673\t      picked.push(await page.evaluate(() => (window as unknown as { hovered: number }).hovered));\n   674\t      // The x scale maps the domain onto the plot's width; the nearest datum to the pointer's position.\n   675\t      const at = xDomain![0]! + (px - box.x) / box.width * (xDomain![1]! - xDomain![0]!);\n   676\t      expected.push(Math.max(0, Math.min(n - 1, Math.round(at))));\n   677\t    }\n   678\t    // Pixel positions are fractional; a datum one index away is the same pixel at this density.\n   679\t    const off = picked.map((p, k) => Math.abs(p - expected[k]!));\n   680\t    expect(Math.max(...off), JSON.stringify({ picked, expected })).toBeLessThanOrEqual(Math.ceil(n / box.width));\n   681\t    expect(new Set(picked).size).toBe(40);\n   682\t    expect(await closePage(page)).toEqual([]);\n   683\t  });\n   684\t});\n   685\t\n   686\tdescribe('spec A5 in the swatches (palette review)', () => {\n   687\t  const eight = ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e', 'f', 'g', 'h'];\n   688\t  const rows = [0, 1, 2].flatMap(x => eight.map((s, i) => ({ x, s, y: 1 + i + x })));\n   689\t\n   690\t  it('eight area series: each legend and tooltip swatch carries that series\\' marker, and the eight are distinct', async () => {\n   691\t    const page = await chartPage({ css: '#chart { width: 900px; }' });\n   692\t    // The markers a line chart's swatches draw are the plotted markers (both come from the view's cues).\n   693\t    await create(page, 'line', { title: 'Lines', data: rows, x: 'x', y: 'y', series: 's' });\n   694\t    const markers = await page.evaluate(() => [...document.querySelectorAll('#chart .axylo-legend-entry svg.axylo-swatch')].map(svg => [...svg.querySelectorAll('path')].at(-1)!.getAttribute('d')));\n   695\t    await page.evaluate(() => window.charts.pop()!.destroy());\n   696\t    await create(page, 'area', { title: 'Areas', data: rows, x: 'x', y: 'y', series: 's' });\n   697\t    const shapes = await page.evaluate(() => [...document.querySelectorAll('#chart .axylo-legend-entry svg.axylo-swatch')].map(svg => ({\n   698\t      chip: !!svg.querySelector('path.axylo-swatch-chip'), marker: svg.querySelector('path.axylo-swatch-marker')?.getAttribute('d') ?? null,\n   699\t    })));\n   700\t    expect(shapes.map(s => s.chip)).toEqual(eight.map(() => true));\n   701\t    expect(shapes.map(s => s.marker)).toEqual(markers);\n   702\t    expect(new Set(markers).size).toBe(8);\n   703\t    const box = (await page.locator('#chart .axylo-proxy').boundingBox())!;\n   704\t    await page.mouse.move(box.x + 2, box.y + box.height / 2);\n   705\t    const tip = await page.evaluate(() => [...document.querySelectorAll('#chart .axylo-tooltip svg.axylo-swatch, #chart .axylo-readout svg.axylo-swatch')].map(svg => svg.querySelector('path.axylo-swatch-marker')?.getAttribute('d') ?? null));\n   706\t    expect(tip).toEqual(markers);\n   707\t    expect(await closePage(page)).toEqual([]);\n   708\t  });\n   709\t\n   710\t  it('bar swatches past the eighth series carry the bar pattern; the first eight do not', async () => {\npackages/axylo/test/browser/a11y/util.ts\npackages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/i1-pointer.browser.test.ts\npackages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/util.ts\npackages/axylo/test/browser/harness.ts\n3:Status: working choice, **not rehearsed with any screen reader**. The plan (M1) called for three candidate patterns to be rehearsed on five screen-reader and browser pairs before choosing. No screen reader can be run in the environment this was built in, so the choice below was made by reasoning and by inspecting Chrome's accessibility tree, and it must be rehearsed by the owner before it is relied on. If rehearsal contradicts it, the plot's roles are internal and can change without touching the public API.\n9:| A. `role=\"application\"` on the plot, arrow keys handled in script, point text sent to a live region | Used by Recharts and ApexCharts. Full control of keys. | Screen-reader authors discourage `application`; a live region is a second, slower channel; touch screen readers have no arrow keys, so points cannot be reached by swiping. |\n10:| B. A roving focusable proxy element per point (or one moved around), each with its own label | Each point is a real element with a name. | One element per point does not scale to 100,000 points; a single moved proxy re-announces its role on every move in some readers (from memory, not verified). |\n11:| C. One native `<input type=\"range\">` laid over the plot, its value the position along x, its `aria-valuetext` the point's text | A native control: desktop readers enter focus mode on it by themselves, arrow keys, Home, End and Page keys work without script, and a change of value text is read without a live region. Touch screen readers expose a native range as an adjustable element, so swiping up and down moves through points (from memory for VoiceOver on iOS and TalkBack; not verified here). One element whatever the number of points. | It is announced as a slider, which is not what a chart is; mitigated by `aria-roledescription` and a label that says what moving it does. Up and Down natively do the same as Right and Left. |\n18:- **Value text**: by default everything at that x, like the pointer tooltip: the x, then each visible series with its value, then \"point 4 of 120\". After the reader narrows to one series (Up or Down on a keyboard), only that series is read. Escape returns to all series.\n19:- **Keys**: Left and Right move along x (native). Home, End, Page Up and Page Down native. Up and Down are intercepted to change the series in focus. `+`, `-`, `0` and Shift+arrows zoom and pan. Enter or Space selects (fires `select`). Escape: closes the tooltip first, then leaves single-series mode; otherwise it is not consumed, so a chart inside a dialog does not swallow it.\n20:- **Touch screen readers**: swiping up or down on the adjustable element moves along x and reads every series at that x; the table is one control away in the toolbar.\n25:1. NVDA with Chrome and with Firefox: focus mode is entered on the range without a keystroke; each arrow press reads the new value text once; Up and Down are passed through and the series change is read.\n27:3. VoiceOver on iOS and TalkBack with Chrome: the element is reachable by touch exploration at the plot's position; swipe up and down move by one point and read it; the toolbar and table are reachable by swiping right.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
